254
00:09:29,034 --> 00:09:30,671
something that lives under a rock.

255
00:09:30,671 --> 00:09:33,248
- Judy, I do wish you would speak English

256
00:09:33,248 --> 00:09:34,633
like normal human beings.

257
00:09:34,633 --> 00:09:35,466
- [Tom] Bitch.

258
00:09:35,466 --> 00:09:38,078
- Besides, I'm sure Mr. Phipps
is a very nice young man.

259
00:09:38,078 --> 00:09:40,930
- I know, just because Bob is a book-gook

260
00:09:40,930 --> 00:09:43,681
that's no reason he has to
bring another one home with him.

261
00:09:43,681 --> 00:09:44,600
- Well, we'll see.

262
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:46,774
Oh, there's a special message for you.

263
00:09:46,774 --> 00:09:48,007
- [Tom] I loathe you.

264
00:09:48,007 --> 00:09:49,431
- And be sure and tell Judy

265
00:09:49,431 --> 00:09:52,543
to keep her grimy little
paws off of my friend.

266
00:09:52,543 --> 00:09:54,770
He's got no time for babies.

267
00:09:54,770 --> 00:09:56,847
- [Mike] He's not Jerry Seinfeld.

268
00:09:56,847 --> 00:09:58,586
- What's his name again?

269
00:09:58,586 --> 00:10:00,447
- [Crow] George Kaplan.

270
00:10:00,447 --> 00:10:02,155
- Alexander Phipps.

271
00:10:02,155 --> 00:10:05,857
- Alexander Phipps, sounds revolting.

272
00:10:05,857 --> 00:10:07,704
- [Tom] Sounds Montclair moment-y

273
00:10:07,704 --> 00:10:09,219
- Whatever made my dear brother

274
00:10:09,219 --> 00:10:10,302
think I'd be interested in a character

275
00:10:10,302 --> 00:10:11,593
with a name like that?

276
00:10:11,593 --> 00:10:12,844
- [Tom] Well, you throw
yourself at anything that--

277
00:10:12,844 --> 00:10:15,119
- As far as Mr. Alexander
Phipps is concerned,

278
00:10:15,119 --> 00:10:17,290
he'll be left alone.

279
00:10:17,290 --> 00:10:19,248
- [Tom] (laughs) Permanently.

280
00:10:19,248 --> 00:10:21,142
- And with the greatest of pleasure.

281
00:10:21,142 --> 00:10:24,046
- [Tom] More food, hide
the pain, more food.

282
00:10:24,046 --> 00:10:25,879
- [Crow] Mmm, Munster.

283
00:10:27,978 --> 00:10:29,656
- [Mike] ♫ But, Patty only sees the sights

284
00:10:29,656 --> 00:10:30,831
♫ A girl can see from Brooklyn Heights

285
00:10:30,831 --> 00:10:33,565
♫ Hey, what a crazy ♫

286
00:10:33,565 --> 00:10:36,025
- [Tom] The young Millie
helper chronicles.

287
00:10:36,025 --> 00:10:38,422
- Judy, isn't it about
time you got dressed, dear?

288
00:10:38,422 --> 00:10:39,721
- I'm dressed.

289
00:10:39,721 --> 00:10:41,601
- Well, that's a matter of opinion.

290
00:10:41,601 --> 00:10:43,669
Go put on a dress before they get here.

291
00:10:43,669 --> 00:10:44,836
- Ah, for him?

292
00:10:45,936 --> 00:10:48,533
Okay, but nobody named Alexander Phipps

293
00:10:48,533 --> 00:10:50,427
would ever notice what I've got on.

294
00:10:50,427 --> 00:10:52,043
- [Crow] Yeah, if you're
named Alexander Phipps,

295
00:10:52,043 --> 00:10:53,841
it's just over for ya.

296
00:10:53,841 --> 00:10:55,451
- [Tom] Wow, the bacon truck is here.

297
00:10:55,451 --> 00:10:57,284
- Jeepers, is he cool.

298
00:10:58,488 --> 00:11:00,321
Really cool.

299
00:11:00,321 --> 00:11:02,125
- [Tom] Tonight's the night, Mom.

300
00:11:02,125 --> 00:11:04,354
Yipe, Mother, they're here.

301
00:11:04,354 --> 00:11:06,315
And look at me, I'm a mess.

302
00:11:06,315 --> 00:11:08,208
- Well, what do you care?

303
00:11:08,208 --> 00:11:11,748
Remember, his name is Alexander Phipps.

304
00:11:11,748 --> 00:11:14,037
- Oh, wait 'til I get that
brother of mine alone.

305
00:11:14,037 --> 00:11:17,793
(bouncy happy music)

306
00:11:17,793 --> 00:11:20,700
- [Tom] Jeez, It's Chip and Dale.

307
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:24,033
- [Crow] Would you just get in the door.

308
00:11:24,910 --> 00:11:25,879
- Hi mom.

309
00:11:25,879 --> 00:11:27,097
- [Mike] I'm back from the 20's.

310
00:11:27,097 --> 00:11:28,444
- Oh gee, it's good to be home.

311
00:11:28,444 --> 00:11:29,690
- [Tom] And to be Robert McNamara.

312
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:31,405
- Mom, this is Alex.

313
00:11:31,405 --> 00:11:32,701
- [Mother] Hello, Alex.

314
00:11:32,701 --> 00:11:34,439
We're very happy to have you with us.

315
00:11:34,439 --> 00:11:35,828
- Oh, thank you, Mrs. Adams.

316
00:11:35,828 --> 00:11:37,344
It's nice of you to have me.

317
00:11:37,344 --> 00:11:38,829
- [Crow] It's nice to be had.

318
00:11:38,829 --> 00:11:40,561
- Wait a minute, don't tell me.

319
00:11:40,561 --> 00:11:42,464
You're the twin without the Tony.

320
00:11:42,464 --> 00:11:45,678
- [Tom] Oh, go sit on
a carrot, you crummy.

321
00:11:45,678 --> 00:11:47,828
- Alex, this is the little
sister I was telling you about.

322
00:11:47,828 --> 00:11:49,768
Judy, Alexander Phipps.

323
00:11:49,768 --> 00:11:51,349
- How do you do, Mr. Phipps.

324
00:11:51,349 --> 00:11:52,919
So nice you could come.

325
00:11:52,919 --> 00:11:54,454
- Well, thanks, Judy.

326
00:11:54,454 --> 00:11:55,816
And the name is Alex.

327
00:11:55,816 --> 00:11:57,186
- [Mike] Zander Phipps.

328
00:11:57,186 --> 00:11:58,569
- Did you boys have your lunch?

329
00:11:58,569 --> 00:12:01,270
- Oh no, we didn't wanna miss
another one of your meals.

330
00:12:01,270 --> 00:12:03,205
Besides, we wanted to catch a shower.

331
00:12:03,205 --> 00:12:04,309
- [All] Whoa!
- [Mike] Look at him.

332
00:12:04,309 --> 00:12:05,171
- Okay, run along then.

333
00:12:05,171 --> 00:12:07,251
I'll have everything ready
by the time you get back.

334
00:12:07,251 --> 00:12:08,885
- [Tom] Join us later, if you want, Ma.

335
00:12:08,885 --> 00:12:11,737
- Oh, Mom, how about some of
those hot biscuits of yours?

336
00:12:11,737 --> 00:12:12,641
- [Mike] Whoa, my god.

337
00:12:12,641 --> 00:12:13,898
- All right, hot biscuits.

338
00:12:13,898 --> 00:12:15,142
Judy will make them for you.

339
00:12:15,142 --> 00:12:17,374
- Judy, I didn't know she could--

340
00:12:17,374 --> 00:12:19,203
- [Crow] Biscuit.

341
00:12:19,203 --> 00:12:20,310
- C'mon, Alex.

342
00:12:20,310 --> 00:12:22,021
- Oh that sounds real good, and thanks.

343
00:12:22,021 --> 00:12:24,425
- [Tom] Hee, I'll soap you first.

344
00:12:24,425 --> 00:12:26,676
- Mother, you do the biscuits, huh.

345
00:12:26,676 --> 00:12:28,582
- Well, it'll only take
you a moment, Judy.

346
00:12:28,582 --> 00:12:29,767
- Thanks, angel.

347
00:12:29,767 --> 00:12:31,385
I think I will throw on a dress.

348
00:12:31,385 --> 00:12:33,178
More ladylike, don't you think so?

349
00:12:33,178 --> 00:12:36,569
- Yes, dear, but Alexander
Phipps will never notice that.

350
00:12:36,569 --> 00:12:38,508
- You're a doll, Mother.

351
00:12:38,508 --> 00:12:40,501
- [Tom] Now make the biscuits.

352
00:12:40,501 --> 00:12:43,356
- [Crow] You can hardly
see where you bit me.

353
00:12:43,356 --> 00:12:45,759
- Sure do get a smooth shave
with this electric razor.

354
00:12:45,759 --> 00:12:46,993
I hope your dad won't mind.

355
00:12:46,993 --> 00:12:49,261
- [Bob] No, he won't mind a bit.

356
00:12:49,261 --> 00:12:51,396
He always lets me use it when I'm home.

357
00:12:51,396 --> 00:12:53,555
Dad's a swell guy, you'll like him.

358
00:12:53,555 --> 00:12:56,436
- [Tom] Just don't use his
razor, he goes ballistic.

359
00:12:56,436 --> 00:13:00,371
- Hey, you're not running
outta hot water, are ya?

360
00:13:00,371 --> 00:13:01,204
- What?

361
00:13:01,204 --> 00:13:03,415
- I said, you're not running
out of hot water, are ya?

362
00:13:03,415 --> 00:13:05,152
I stayed under quite a long time.

363
00:13:05,152 --> 00:13:05,985
- [Mike] Wow.

364
00:13:05,985 --> 00:13:06,818
- Aw, don't worry about that

365
00:13:06,818 --> 00:13:09,437
Wait 'til ya see the electric
water heater Dad got us.

366
00:13:09,437 --> 00:13:10,405
Full loaded, now.

367
00:13:10,405 --> 00:13:11,967
We have plenty of hot water all the time.

368
00:13:11,967 --> 00:13:13,507
- [Crow] Sounds fun.

369
00:13:13,507 --> 00:13:15,399
I gotta get outta here.

370
00:13:15,399 --> 00:13:16,328
- Hey, Bob.

371
00:13:16,328 --> 00:13:17,237
- [Bob] Yeah.

372
00:13:17,237 --> 00:13:19,519
- I thought you said your
sister was just a little kid.

373
00:13:19,519 --> 00:13:20,709
- [Tom] She must be 40.

374
00:13:20,709 --> 00:13:23,349
- I didn't wanna fool ya,
she's still a little monster.

375
00:13:23,349 --> 00:13:26,099
- [Tom] And I'm the Beast Master.

376
00:13:27,515 --> 00:13:30,134
- [Crow] There, now I look like Mom.

377
00:13:30,134 --> 00:13:34,051
Well thank goodness for my electric dress.

378
00:13:34,051 --> 00:13:37,523
- [Tom] Where's the fire extinguisher?

379
00:13:37,523 --> 00:13:40,866
- [Mike] He'll never touch
you, Terry, you're dirt.

380
00:13:40,866 --> 00:13:42,838
- Hey, you look mighty purty.

381
00:13:42,838 --> 00:13:44,368
- [Mike] In a Thelma Ritter sorta way.

382
00:13:44,368 --> 00:13:45,795
- How about that food?

383
00:13:45,795 --> 00:13:49,050
- Food, is that all you can
think of at a time like this?

384
00:13:49,050 --> 00:13:50,054
- [Crow] You mean lunchtime?

385
00:13:50,054 --> 00:13:51,752
- Don't change the subject.

386
00:13:51,752 --> 00:13:55,053
I ought to ring your smug little neck.

387
00:13:55,053 --> 00:13:56,239
- What's the matter now?

388
00:13:56,239 --> 00:13:57,349
What have I done?

389
00:13:57,349 --> 00:13:59,760
- Studious, serious minded.

390
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:01,842
- What are you talking about?

391
00:14:01,842 --> 00:14:03,287
- Tops in all his classes.

392
00:14:03,287 --> 00:14:04,491
- [Tom] Point, counterpoint.

393
00:14:04,491 --> 00:14:07,180
- Now, Judy, Alex is really a guy who--

394
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:08,305
- Had little time for girls.

395
00:14:08,305 --> 00:14:09,899
- Now, wait a minute, let me explain.

396
00:14:09,899 --> 00:14:12,083
- And a woman hater to boot.

397
00:14:12,083 --> 00:14:14,818
I think you're a stinker.

398
00:14:14,818 --> 00:14:15,940
- [Crow] No, don't.

399
00:14:15,940 --> 00:14:17,799
- Why do girls have to have brothers?

400
00:14:17,799 --> 00:14:19,326
- I love you, too.

401
00:14:19,326 --> 00:14:21,058
Now, simmer down, sis.

402
00:14:21,058 --> 00:14:22,188
It's the truth.

403
00:14:22,188 --> 00:14:24,958
Alex really has no time for girls.

404
00:14:24,958 --> 00:14:27,015
Especially you.

405
00:14:27,015 --> 00:14:30,414
- Flattery will get you
no where, just watch.

406
00:14:30,414 --> 00:14:33,991
- [Tom] Do, do, dum,
god I hate women, oh hi.

407
00:14:33,991 --> 00:14:35,384
- Hi Alex, I know you're hungry.

408
00:14:35,384 --> 00:14:37,587
You'll be eating in a jiffy.

409
00:14:37,587 --> 00:14:39,837
- [Crow] Ow, my new kidney.

410
00:14:43,426 --> 00:14:44,890
- [Mike] Oh, one of them in-sink-a-raters

411
00:14:44,890 --> 00:14:45,881
- [Mother] Everything's ready, Judy

412
00:14:45,881 --> 00:14:47,531
- Come and get it.

413
00:14:47,531 --> 00:14:51,903
- [Tom] (sputters and gags)

414
00:14:51,903 --> 00:14:53,875
- [Crow] Hey, make with the chow, broads.

415
00:14:53,875 --> 00:14:55,324
- C'mon Alex, make yourself at home.

416
00:14:55,324 --> 00:14:57,126
- Sorry to put you to all
this trouble, Mrs. Adams.

417
00:14:57,126 --> 00:14:58,881
But, it does smell good.

418
00:14:58,881 --> 00:15:00,408
- No trouble at all, Alex.

419
00:15:00,408 --> 00:15:03,509
Everything is so easy
with an electric range.

420
00:15:03,509 --> 00:15:04,706
Possibly, I'm prejudiced,

421
00:15:04,706 --> 00:15:07,528
but even the food tastes better, too.

422
00:15:07,528 --> 00:15:09,040
- Say, I hate to be corny beautiful,

423
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:10,113
but what's cooking?

424
00:15:10,113 --> 00:15:11,613
- Stew, beef stew.

425
00:15:12,506 --> 00:15:14,921
Mom knows how you love it.

426
00:15:14,921 --> 00:15:16,791
- It's wonderful, it's my favorite dish.

427
00:15:16,791 --> 00:15:19,072
- [Tom] Well, used to be my favorite dish.

428
00:15:19,072 --> 00:15:20,849
- [Mike] Jesus, do you got a claw hammer

429
00:15:20,849 --> 00:15:22,918
or something I can work on this with?

430
00:15:22,918 --> 00:15:24,201
- Hot biscuits.

431
00:15:24,201 --> 00:15:26,129
Mother made them.

432
00:15:26,129 --> 00:15:27,680
- [Crow] Hey, the electric phone.

433
00:15:27,680 --> 00:15:29,124
- I'll get it, mom.

434
00:15:29,124 --> 00:15:30,837
- Try some of these (mumbles).

435
00:15:30,837 --> 00:15:32,275
- Thanks.

436
00:15:32,275 --> 00:15:33,649
- [Tom] Dad's calling from Chippendale's.

437
00:15:33,649 --> 00:15:34,482
He got two shows.

438
00:15:34,482 --> 00:15:35,899
He won't be home.

439
00:15:37,188 --> 00:15:39,163
More Similac, dear?

440
00:15:39,163 --> 00:15:40,490
- Did he arrive?

441
00:15:40,490 --> 00:15:42,668
Man, he's positively frantic.

442
00:15:42,668 --> 00:15:43,935
- [Crow] He runs around screaming.

443
00:15:43,935 --> 00:15:45,567
- Real cool Jonah.

444
00:15:45,567 --> 00:15:47,573
Tall, good looking

445
00:15:47,573 --> 00:15:49,378
- [Mike] Spit out by a whale.

446
00:15:49,378 --> 00:15:51,021
- Nothing at all like
the drips around school.

447
00:15:51,021 --> 00:15:52,104
- [Tom] If you'd like to make a call,

448
00:15:52,104 --> 00:15:52,985
please hang up and dial again.

449
00:15:52,985 --> 00:15:56,914
- Uh-huh, he drives a
real shafty convertible.

450
00:15:56,914 --> 00:15:57,991
He really has it.

451
00:15:57,991 --> 00:15:59,092
- [Tom] And I want it.

452
00:15:59,092 --> 00:16:02,835
- And when he looks at me,
I get, you know, squishy.

453
00:16:02,835 --> 00:16:03,668
- [Crow] Well, that's nice, ma'am.

454
00:16:03,668 --> 00:16:04,654
I'm just trying to sell my magazines.

455
00:16:04,654 --> 00:16:07,218
- This afternoon, oh
that's a wonderful idea.

456
00:16:07,218 --> 00:16:08,613
- [Crow] Have my way with him?

457
00:16:08,613 --> 00:16:10,530
- What time, okay, bye.

458
00:16:11,447 --> 00:16:14,019
- That stew was really a
production, Mrs. Adams.

459
00:16:14,019 --> 00:16:15,170
Can we help with the dishes?

460
00:16:15,170 --> 00:16:17,799
- Nah-ah, No KP duty around here, Sargent.

461
00:16:17,799 --> 00:16:20,303
- [Tom] We got dames to do that.

462
00:16:20,303 --> 00:16:22,470
- Strictly automatic, see?

463
00:16:24,490 --> 00:16:25,330
- Whoops.

464
00:16:25,330 --> 00:16:26,163
- [Tom] Found the smell.

465
00:16:26,163 --> 00:16:27,226
- They're in here all ready, mom.

466
00:16:27,226 --> 00:16:29,322
- That's all right, dear, just
put them in with the others.

467
00:16:29,322 --> 00:16:30,551
- [Crow] Why, what are
all these gin bottles

468
00:16:30,551 --> 00:16:31,718
doing in here?

469
00:16:33,308 --> 00:16:35,721
- Oh, wait a minute, Bob,
why not do it the easy way.

470
00:16:35,721 --> 00:16:37,110
- [Tom] You're suffocating me.

471
00:16:37,110 --> 00:16:38,301
- Excuse me mom.

472
00:16:38,301 --> 00:16:39,564
- [Crow] It's Pepperidge Farm.

473
00:16:39,564 --> 00:16:41,308
- That used to be my job at home.

474
00:16:41,308 --> 00:16:43,150
Washing the dishes, I mean.

475
00:16:43,150 --> 00:16:45,758
- Oh, Bob and Judy have
had their share, too.

476
00:16:45,758 --> 00:16:48,047
But thank heaven, no
more washing and wiping

477
00:16:48,047 --> 00:16:49,095
dishes around here.

478
00:16:49,095 --> 00:16:50,811
- [Tom] God, your hair smells terrific

479
00:16:50,811 --> 00:16:52,650
- And the dishwasher uses such hot water,

480
00:16:52,650 --> 00:16:55,243
you couldn't possibly
put your hands in it.

481
00:16:55,243 --> 00:16:59,328
- Sure, that makes the
dishes hygienically clean.

482
00:16:59,328 --> 00:17:00,930
- I'll put the deep well cooker
in when I do the next load.

483
00:17:00,930 --> 00:17:03,097
- [Crow] I've never felt so
comfortable with a woman,

484
00:17:03,097 --> 00:17:04,157
Mrs. Adams.

485
00:17:04,157 --> 00:17:05,583
- [Tom] Alexander, please.

486
00:17:05,583 --> 00:17:06,874
- [Crow] Do you wanna
have breakfast sometime?

487
00:17:06,874 --> 00:17:10,124
- [Tom] Don't do this to me, Alexander.

488
00:17:12,248 --> 00:17:13,619
- See, nothing to it, is there?

489
00:17:13,619 --> 00:17:15,694
- [Tom] And it's quiet, too.

490
00:17:15,694 --> 00:17:16,527
- What?

491
00:17:16,527 --> 00:17:18,773
- [Crow] Eleanor, I'm busy
down here at the White House.

492
00:17:18,773 --> 00:17:23,312
- Okay, beautiful, and don't
forget to call me, will ya?

493
00:17:23,312 --> 00:17:25,175
- [Crow] Bye, Eleanor.

494
00:17:25,175 --> 00:17:27,618
- Sally phoned and asked
me to go for a swim.

495
00:17:27,618 --> 00:17:29,562
Thought you might like to come along.

496
00:17:29,562 --> 00:17:31,533
And bring Alex.

497
00:17:31,533 --> 00:17:34,938
- Oh, thanks, but I think
Alex is going to be busy.

498
00:17:34,938 --> 00:17:36,907
- Busy doing what?

499
00:17:36,907 --> 00:17:39,990
- Now look, small fry,
don't gimme any trouble.

500
00:17:39,990 --> 00:17:42,274
All I know is that seeing a man at the

501
00:17:42,274 --> 00:17:45,473
northwest machine works
on a matter of hydraulics.

502
00:17:45,473 --> 00:17:47,141
- Why don't you ask him, yourself?

503
00:17:47,141 --> 00:17:48,716
- Thanks, I will.

504
00:17:48,716 --> 00:17:53,147
- [Tom] I'm squishy, and
I need to move on it.

505
00:17:53,147 --> 00:17:55,258
- And the circulating air
completely dries them.

506
00:17:55,258 --> 00:17:56,602
- [Tom] Please, stop, I'm vulnerable.

507
00:17:56,602 --> 00:17:58,252
- You certainly know
all about dish washers.

508
00:17:58,252 --> 00:17:59,297
- Oh, not all.

509
00:17:59,297 --> 00:18:01,673
But to tell the truth, Mrs.
Adams, anything of a mechanical,

510
00:18:01,673 --> 00:18:03,669
or electrical nature interests me.

511
00:18:03,669 --> 00:18:05,793
Fits in with a hobby of mine.

512
00:18:05,793 --> 00:18:06,966
- Dishwashers?

513
00:18:06,966 --> 00:18:08,958
- Not exactly, time study.

514
00:18:08,958 --> 00:18:10,522
Figuring out how to do things better.

515
00:18:10,522 --> 00:18:13,781
(Tom makes trombone noises)

516
00:18:13,781 --> 00:18:15,955
- Time study sounds interesting.

517
00:18:15,955 --> 00:18:18,266
- And does going for a swim
sound interesting, Alex.

518
00:18:18,266 --> 00:18:19,764
It's a swell day for us.

519
00:18:19,764 --> 00:18:21,334
- It is at that, Judy.

520
00:18:21,334 --> 00:18:22,199
And it would be great, but--

521
00:18:22,199 --> 00:18:23,716
- [Tom] I find you repulsive.

522
00:18:23,716 --> 00:18:24,715
- Can we make it some other time?

523
00:18:24,715 --> 00:18:25,905
I have an appointment this afternoon.

524
00:18:25,905 --> 00:18:28,330
With an engineer, as a matter of fact,

525
00:18:28,330 --> 00:18:30,615
that's one of the reasons,
I came home with Bob.

526
00:18:30,615 --> 00:18:32,113
- [Tom] One of the reasons, heh-heh.

527
00:18:32,113 --> 00:18:34,094
- I'd better hurry, too.

528
00:18:34,094 --> 00:18:36,101
Thanks for the lunch, Mrs. Adams.

529
00:18:36,101 --> 00:18:37,175
- [Crow] Just please go.

530
00:18:37,175 --> 00:18:38,244
- Have a good swim, Judy.

531
00:18:38,244 --> 00:18:41,050
- [Crow] Sure I will, homo.

532
00:18:41,050 --> 00:18:44,550
(upbeat electronic music)

533
00:18:48,171 --> 00:18:50,291
- A lecture about mushrooms.

534
00:18:50,291 --> 00:18:51,601
- [Crow] You're dating Timothy Leery?

535
00:18:51,601 --> 00:18:52,803
- Must be some mechanical problem

536
00:18:52,803 --> 00:18:56,155
connected with the lecture,
if Alex wants to go.

537
00:18:56,155 --> 00:18:57,919
- [Tom] God, I wish he'd come home.

538
00:18:57,919 --> 00:18:59,182
Cockroach, sorry.

539
00:18:59,182 --> 00:19:02,158
- Growing mushrooms under
artificial conditions.

540
00:19:02,158 --> 00:19:02,991
- [Mike] I like it.

541
00:19:02,991 --> 00:19:05,167
Well, after all, the lecture
may be very interesting.

542
00:19:05,167 --> 00:19:06,299
- Yes, mother.

543
00:19:06,299 --> 00:19:08,525
- [Crow] We should leave
the house sometime, too.

544
00:19:08,525 --> 00:19:09,881
- Mechanical problem.

545
00:19:09,881 --> 00:19:10,730
- [Tom] Mom

546
00:19:10,730 --> 00:19:11,640
- Time study.

547
00:19:11,640 --> 00:19:13,156
- [Tom] Earth to Mom.

548
00:19:13,156 --> 00:19:14,300
- What did you say, mother?

549
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:16,339
- Judy, I have an idea.

550
00:19:16,339 --> 00:19:18,555
- Yes, I remember your father.

551
00:19:18,555 --> 00:19:20,374
- [Crow] Vaguely.

552
00:19:20,374 --> 00:19:21,812
- The trouble I had with him.

553
00:19:21,812 --> 00:19:23,709
- [Tom] He was a thumb sucker.

554
00:19:23,709 --> 00:19:26,209
- Seems as if nothing I did
would interest him until I--

555
00:19:26,209 --> 00:19:27,333
- Until what, Mother.

556
00:19:27,333 --> 00:19:28,196
- [Crow] I got my tattoo.

557
00:19:28,196 --> 00:19:30,207
- I discovered how much
he enjoyed good food.

558
00:19:30,207 --> 00:19:31,770
- Oh.

559
00:19:31,770 --> 00:19:33,308
- [Mother] The way to a man's heart, Judy.

560
00:19:33,308 --> 00:19:34,964
- Oh Mother, that's corny.

561
00:19:34,964 --> 00:19:37,117
Everyone likes good food.

562
00:19:37,117 --> 00:19:39,877
- I know, dear, but I'm
thinking of something else, too.

563
00:19:39,877 --> 00:19:40,876
- [Mike] Nude housework.

564
00:19:40,876 --> 00:19:42,397
- It'll take a little conspiracy.

565
00:19:42,397 --> 00:19:43,443
- [Judy] Conspiracy?

566
00:19:43,443 --> 00:19:45,092
A nice little conspiracy,

567
00:19:45,092 --> 00:19:46,304
for a nice young man.

568
00:19:46,304 --> 00:19:47,561
- [Tom] Like Whitewater.

569
00:19:47,561 --> 00:19:49,621
- Remember I decided not
to go to the garden club

570
00:19:49,621 --> 00:19:52,242
dinner tomorrow night,
because of Alexander's visit?

571
00:19:52,242 --> 00:19:53,645
Well, I changed my mind.

572
00:19:53,645 --> 00:19:54,494
I'm going.

573
00:19:54,494 --> 00:19:55,461
- [Crow] To hell with Alexander.

574
00:19:55,461 --> 00:19:58,096
- You can find out for yourself
why the mother knows best.

575
00:19:58,096 --> 00:19:59,288
Now, here's what we'll do.

576
00:19:59,288 --> 00:20:00,837
- [Crow] Get some rope, a chain saw.

577
00:20:00,837 --> 00:20:02,693
- [Tom] Oh, I like that.

578
00:20:02,693 --> 00:20:04,439
- [Crow] Potassium nitrate, sulfur iodide.

579
00:20:04,439 --> 00:20:05,575
- [Tom] ♫ Going on a man

580
00:20:05,575 --> 00:20:09,381
♫ Hunt get me a man, de-de-de-de-de ♫

581
00:20:09,381 --> 00:20:11,964
Okay, and thumbtacks.

582
00:20:11,964 --> 00:20:15,714
(Tom hums theme from "Jaws")

583
00:20:18,650 --> 00:20:20,658
- [Crow] Why I'm baking my panties,

584
00:20:20,658 --> 00:20:23,849
but that's what mom suggested.

585
00:20:23,849 --> 00:20:28,016
- [Tom] I'll turn it on
now, but I light it later.

586
00:20:29,726 --> 00:20:30,559
- [Mike] Here you go, Daddy.

587
00:20:30,559 --> 00:20:32,239
- [Tom] Thanks, kitten (gurgles).

588
00:20:32,239 --> 00:20:33,754
- Do you need anything
before I leave, Judy.

589
00:20:33,754 --> 00:20:35,712
- I don't think so, Mother.

590
00:20:35,712 --> 00:20:36,745
Now, how long did you say

591
00:20:36,745 --> 00:20:38,129
it would take this dinner to cook?

592
00:20:38,129 --> 00:20:39,416
- Now, let's see, your dinner should cook

593
00:20:39,416 --> 00:20:41,952
for an hour and a half at 350 degrees.

594
00:20:41,952 --> 00:20:44,135
- (mumbles) Sweetie, let me
make with the mathematics.

595
00:20:44,135 --> 00:20:46,072
I'm gonna dash over to Sally's.

596
00:20:46,072 --> 00:20:47,507
She's gonna set my hair.

597
00:20:47,507 --> 00:20:49,659
- It's almost three o'clock now.

598
00:20:49,659 --> 00:20:52,361
You will eat at 6:30,
and your dinner takes

599
00:20:52,361 --> 00:20:53,759
an hour and a half to cook.

600
00:20:53,759 --> 00:20:55,258
- [Crow] The naughtys
will be on you by then.

601
00:20:55,258 --> 00:20:57,752
- [Judy] Set the timer.

602
00:20:57,752 --> 00:20:59,053
- [Tom] The timing bombs will be

603
00:20:59,053 --> 00:21:01,335
tripped by the wires
when we drive through.

604
00:21:01,335 --> 00:21:02,889
- And to cook for an hour and a half.

605
00:21:02,889 --> 00:21:04,391
- [Mike] They forgot to stop filming.

606
00:21:04,391 --> 00:21:05,741
- Then I set the flip switch,

607
00:21:05,741 --> 00:21:07,406
so it will go on automatically.

608
00:21:07,406 --> 00:21:09,027
Set temperature dial to--

609
00:21:09,027 --> 00:21:10,685
- [Crow] I think this is cool.

610
00:21:10,685 --> 00:21:13,515
- 350, and I'm off to Sally's, okay?

611
00:21:13,515 --> 00:21:15,266
- Absolutely right.

612
00:21:15,266 --> 00:21:16,811
- Glad I remembered to take the ham

613
00:21:16,811 --> 00:21:18,962
out of the freezer this morning.

614
00:21:18,962 --> 00:21:20,420
- [Tom] Is Mom in the Guardian Angles?

615
00:21:20,420 --> 00:21:21,441
- [Crow] Yeah.

616
00:21:21,441 --> 00:21:22,673
- I wish you weren't going out.

617
00:21:22,673 --> 00:21:23,948
Do you think everything will be all right?

618
00:21:23,948 --> 00:21:26,115
- Of course, darling, it'll be lovely.

619
00:21:26,115 --> 00:21:27,191
Now, don't you worry.

620
00:21:27,191 --> 00:21:29,756
This isn't the first meal
you've cooked, you know.

621
00:21:29,756 --> 00:21:32,141
- I know, but it's the
first meal I ever cooked

622
00:21:32,141 --> 00:21:32,974
- [Crow] Uh-oh.

623
00:21:32,974 --> 00:21:33,923
- For Alex.

624
00:21:33,923 --> 00:21:34,948
- [Tom] She's getting squishy.

625
00:21:34,948 --> 00:21:35,943
- [All] Bleh!

626
00:21:35,943 --> 00:21:39,702
- Alex, he'll be pleasantly
surprised, and impressed.

627
00:21:39,702 --> 00:21:42,549
I have to hurry, dear,
I have things to do.

628
00:21:42,549 --> 00:21:44,513
- What's a four letter word for nuance?

629
00:21:44,513 --> 00:21:48,680
- [Crow] Standard sitcom
joke, landing on runway seven.

630
00:21:49,903 --> 00:21:52,266
- That's easy, J-u-d-y.

631
00:21:52,266 --> 00:21:53,746
- [Mike] Yes (laughs).

632
00:21:53,746 --> 00:21:55,174
- Where are you going, mom?

633
00:21:55,174 --> 00:21:56,850
- Can we give you a lift, Mrs. Adams?

634
00:21:56,850 --> 00:21:57,949
- No thanks, boys.

635
00:21:57,949 --> 00:21:59,583
I don't know what time
I'll be back tonight.

636
00:21:59,583 --> 00:22:01,265
So, I'm taking the car.

637
00:22:01,265 --> 00:22:03,313
- [Bob] Tonight, what about dinner?

638
00:22:03,313 --> 00:22:04,939
- Oh, Judy's taking care of that.

639
00:22:04,939 --> 00:22:06,523
- Judy?

640
00:22:06,523 --> 00:22:09,074
Why Judy can't even boil
water without burning it.

641
00:22:09,074 --> 00:22:10,231
(loud laughs)

642
00:22:10,231 --> 00:22:11,644
Maybe we oughta go to Hamburger Harry's.

643
00:22:11,644 --> 00:22:13,513
At least we'll get something to eat.

644
00:22:13,513 --> 00:22:15,887
- Go ahead, but you'll be sorry.

645
00:22:15,887 --> 00:22:17,128
- Why, I wouldn't think of missing

646
00:22:17,128 --> 00:22:18,359
one of Judy's dinners.

647
00:22:18,359 --> 00:22:19,236
- Thank you, Alex.

648
00:22:19,236 --> 00:22:22,171
- [Tom] Let's get in
the car, let's go quick.

649
00:22:22,171 --> 00:22:23,918
- You shouldn't tease her so, dear.

650
00:22:23,918 --> 00:22:25,768
- Aw, Mom, I was only needling her.

651
00:22:25,768 --> 00:22:27,231
- Well, don't over do it.

652
00:22:27,231 --> 00:22:28,080
You'll give her the reds.

653
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:29,175
- [Crow] What?

654
00:22:29,175 --> 00:22:31,140
She's squishy, she's got the reds.

655
00:22:31,140 --> 00:22:33,473
What's going on around here?

656
00:22:34,460 --> 00:22:36,960
- [Tom] Your mother's a freak.

657
00:22:37,931 --> 00:22:40,247
- [Crow] Hash brownies look good.

658
00:22:40,247 --> 00:22:42,987
- [Tom] A young Mrs.
Lockhorn prepares dinner.

659
00:22:42,987 --> 00:22:44,404
Do-do-do-do-do-do

660
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:47,917
Pert, pert, pert, pert, pert.

661
00:22:48,889 --> 00:22:50,365
The Loretta Young show.

662
00:22:50,365 --> 00:22:51,739
- That's really interesting, Alex.

663
00:22:51,739 --> 00:22:53,562
You know, mushrooms are
big favorites of mine.

664
00:22:53,562 --> 00:22:55,345
- [Mike] 'Shrooms, whooh!

665
00:22:55,345 --> 00:22:57,080
- There's quite an air
conditioning problem--

666
00:22:57,080 --> 00:22:58,507
- [Tom] Is this a Noel Coward play?

667
00:22:58,507 --> 00:23:00,745
- Oh, I don't think that'll
be too much of a problem.

668
00:23:00,745 --> 00:23:03,495
- [Crow] When are you going home?

669
00:23:04,753 --> 00:23:07,924
- [Tom] That's what I need, an idea.

670
00:23:07,924 --> 00:23:11,834
Now, to get my bustier out of the freezer.

671
00:23:11,834 --> 00:23:14,053
(bangs glass bowl)

672
00:23:14,053 --> 00:23:17,150
- [Crow] Has anybody noticed
that the daughter is psychotic?

673
00:23:17,150 --> 00:23:21,150
- Well, you can always
give it a try, Mr. Adams.

674
00:23:22,287 --> 00:23:24,320
- [Mike] What the?
- [Crow] Who?

675
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:25,627
- [Tom] This is like
Three Days of the Condor.

676
00:23:25,627 --> 00:23:28,044
I trust no one in this short.

677
00:23:33,126 --> 00:23:34,543
- [Crow] Oh dear.

678
00:23:37,195 --> 00:23:39,304
- Oh me, dinner will be ruined.

679
00:23:39,304 --> 00:23:41,663
- [Crow] Shut up, the
men are talking in here.

680
00:23:41,663 --> 00:23:43,496
- [Judy] Oh, I don't know what I'll do.

681
00:23:43,496 --> 00:23:44,507
- Oh, excuse me, sir.

682
00:23:44,507 --> 00:23:45,504
Lady in distress.

683
00:23:45,504 --> 00:23:46,337
- Sure.

684
00:23:46,337 --> 00:23:48,378
- [Tom] Heave to, my friend.

685
00:23:48,378 --> 00:23:49,211
- [Alex] Trouble, Judy?

686
00:23:49,211 --> 00:23:50,099
Can I help?

687
00:23:50,099 --> 00:23:51,759
- It's the mixer, it just won't work.

688
00:23:51,759 --> 00:23:53,058
- Well, let me take a look.

689
00:23:53,058 --> 00:23:54,355
- [Tom] Talk to your dad about mushrooms.

690
00:23:54,355 --> 00:23:57,324
He'll think I have breasts, the old fart.

691
00:23:57,324 --> 00:23:59,833
- There we are.
- [Tom] God, you're dumb.

692
00:23:59,833 --> 00:24:00,885
- The plug slipped out, that's all.

693
00:24:00,885 --> 00:24:02,654
- How do you suppose that happened?

694
00:24:02,654 --> 00:24:03,487
- [Tom] Duh.

695
00:24:03,487 --> 00:24:04,659
- Just one of those things.

696
00:24:04,659 --> 00:24:06,252
First thing you do when
an appliance doesn't work

697
00:24:06,252 --> 00:24:07,810
is find out if it's plugged in.

698
00:24:07,810 --> 00:24:09,392
That's the trouble, most of the time.

699
00:24:09,392 --> 00:24:10,943
- [Mike] There.
- [Crow] Good point.

700
00:24:10,943 --> 00:24:12,016
- That what time study is?

701
00:24:12,016 --> 00:24:14,734
- No, what, are you
interested in time study?

702
00:24:14,734 --> 00:24:16,637
- Mother was telling me that--

703
00:24:16,637 --> 00:24:18,403
(Crew make airplane zooming noises)

704
00:24:18,403 --> 00:24:19,522
- It's a hobby of mine.

705
00:24:19,522 --> 00:24:21,342
- [Tom] Set the hook.

706
00:24:21,342 --> 00:24:22,960
Play him, plenty of line.

707
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:24,394
- Take your kitchen.

708
00:24:24,394 --> 00:24:25,715
Ever noticed anything about it?

709
00:24:25,715 --> 00:24:26,838
- [Tom] It's a prison?

710
00:24:26,838 --> 00:24:28,602
- There's the electric
range, the electric sink,

711
00:24:28,602 --> 00:24:30,934
the garbage disposer,
- [Tom] Electric.

712
00:24:30,934 --> 00:24:31,767
- The dishwasher
- [Tom] Electric

713
00:24:31,767 --> 00:24:32,898
- The refrigerator
- [Tom] Electric.

714
00:24:32,898 --> 00:24:34,232
- And the freezer.
- [Tom] Electric.

715
00:24:34,232 --> 00:24:36,402
- Exactly, all wonderful helps.

716
00:24:36,402 --> 00:24:37,522
Now, here's something you probably

717
00:24:37,522 --> 00:24:38,847
never thought about, Judy.

718
00:24:38,847 --> 00:24:41,582
The kitchen today is like
a factory in a lot of ways.

719
00:24:41,582 --> 00:24:42,898
- [Crow] Where's your upper lip?

720
00:24:42,898 --> 00:24:44,186
- Appliance can only be good
if the right tools are used.

721
00:24:44,186 --> 00:24:45,833
- [Crow] Yeah, but your upper lip.

722
00:24:45,833 --> 00:24:46,666
Where did?

723
00:24:46,666 --> 00:24:47,933
- Best machine that money can buy.

724
00:24:47,933 --> 00:24:49,327
- [Crow] Have you considered braces?

725
00:24:49,327 --> 00:24:51,127
- As a result, the work is done faster,

726
00:24:51,127 --> 00:24:54,559
better, and easier, just as
it is in a modern kitchen.

727
00:24:54,559 --> 00:24:55,885
See what I mean?

728
00:24:55,885 --> 00:24:58,915
- Oh yes, and you're so
smart about all these things.

729
00:24:58,915 --> 00:24:59,965
- Not really, Judy.

730
00:24:59,965 --> 00:25:01,410
It's just that I'm interested,

731
00:25:01,410 --> 00:25:03,313
and like to make a study of them.

732
00:25:03,313 --> 00:25:04,616
- [Tom] Now, let's get those clothes off.

733
00:25:04,616 --> 00:25:06,285
- Now here's another thing.

734
00:25:06,285 --> 00:25:08,378
- Not only are the best tools important.

735
00:25:08,378 --> 00:25:10,315
But, everything connected with their use

736
00:25:10,315 --> 00:25:11,749
is taken into consideration.

737
00:25:11,749 --> 00:25:15,403
- [Crow] Don't wag your
finger at me, pink boy.

738
00:25:15,403 --> 00:25:17,536
- Just like machines in our factories.

739
00:25:17,536 --> 00:25:18,369
- [Tom] He's heatin' up.

740
00:25:18,369 --> 00:25:19,384
- Straight line production.

741
00:25:19,384 --> 00:25:20,811
- [Tom] He's in the zone.

742
00:25:20,811 --> 00:25:22,665
- They're not set this way just for looks.

743
00:25:22,665 --> 00:25:23,498
- [Mike] Wow.

744
00:25:23,498 --> 00:25:25,519
- They're placed this way
for a definite purpose.

745
00:25:25,519 --> 00:25:29,228
To save work, time, and unnecessary steps.

746
00:25:29,228 --> 00:25:32,116
- Golly, I'm so impressed.

747
00:25:32,116 --> 00:25:33,271
- [Tom] Needs to meet a musician.

748
00:25:33,271 --> 00:25:34,387
- [Mike] Yeah.

749
00:25:34,387 --> 00:25:35,815
- It's called, time and motion study.

750
00:25:35,815 --> 00:25:36,648
Come here.

751
00:25:36,648 --> 00:25:37,918
- [Crow] Bring that sweet
behind over this way.

752
00:25:37,918 --> 00:25:39,490
Let me work on that zipper.

753
00:25:39,490 --> 00:25:40,826
- Take your home freezer.

754
00:25:40,826 --> 00:25:41,659
- [Crow] Get in.

755
00:25:41,659 --> 00:25:43,383
- Full of fresh frozen foods.

756
00:25:43,383 --> 00:25:45,780
Well, there's a definite
saving of time and money.

