00:53:09,082 --> 00:53:11,765
- [Crow] After Casey Stengel, of course.

1500
00:53:11,765 --> 00:53:13,347
- I figure it this way, Dad.

1501
00:53:13,347 --> 00:53:14,680
- [Tom] Barbie doll.

1502
00:53:14,680 --> 00:53:15,880
- [Mike] Hector Berlioz?

1503
00:53:15,880 --> 00:53:17,709
- You get the low down
almost as soon as it happens.

1504
00:53:17,709 --> 00:53:19,244
- Guess you're right about that.

1505
00:53:19,244 --> 00:53:20,835
What is it you want me to do?

1506
00:53:20,835 --> 00:53:22,146
- That's easy.

1507
00:53:22,146 --> 00:53:24,212
As soon as anything happens, you tell me.

1508
00:53:24,212 --> 00:53:25,061
- [Dad] Everything?

1509
00:53:25,061 --> 00:53:25,912
- Everything.

1510
00:53:25,912 --> 00:53:27,297
- [Tom] There's a ten spot in it for you.

1511
00:53:27,297 --> 00:53:30,023
- So what, it's not to big for you.

1512
00:53:30,023 --> 00:53:32,177
I hardly catch you at home anymore.

1513
00:53:32,177 --> 00:53:33,494
So, I'll have to see you here.

1514
00:53:33,494 --> 00:53:34,327
- [Mike] Ouch.

1515
00:53:34,327 --> 00:53:37,269
- Well, if it'll help you
to be president, I'll do it.

1516
00:53:37,269 --> 00:53:39,008
- Dad, you're a peach.

1517
00:53:39,008 --> 00:53:41,372
Now, anything new on last night's bunch?

1518
00:53:41,372 --> 00:53:42,205
- Have you read the paper?

1519
00:53:42,205 --> 00:53:43,038
- Yes.

1520
00:53:43,038 --> 00:53:43,957
- Then you have the story.

1521
00:53:43,957 --> 00:53:44,817
- Honest.

1522
00:53:44,817 --> 00:53:45,943
- Honest.

1523
00:53:45,943 --> 00:53:48,180
- [Mike] C'mon you write
tappy dick for godsake.

1524
00:53:48,180 --> 00:53:50,957
- Either the girls have
no previous records,

1525
00:53:50,957 --> 00:53:52,732
or they're from out of town.

1526
00:53:52,732 --> 00:53:54,360
It's Lieutenant Holmes theory

1527
00:53:54,360 --> 00:53:56,056
that they were from out of town.

1528
00:53:56,056 --> 00:53:56,889
- [Crow] No we weren't, ooh.

1529
00:53:56,889 --> 00:53:58,268
- Boys can't identify any of them

1530
00:53:58,268 --> 00:53:59,382
from the police pictures files.

1531
00:53:59,382 --> 00:54:03,482
And there it will stay until
something else turns up.

1532
00:54:03,482 --> 00:54:05,021
- What about the gas station affair,

1533
00:54:05,021 --> 00:54:06,392
is there anything new on that?

1534
00:54:06,392 --> 00:54:07,329
- [Tom] Say please.

1535
00:54:07,329 --> 00:54:09,463
- Nothing you could call new.

1536
00:54:09,463 --> 00:54:10,451
Four young boys.

1537
00:54:10,451 --> 00:54:11,536
- [Crow] The Dave Clark Five?

1538
00:54:11,536 --> 00:54:12,369
Oh.

1539
00:54:12,369 --> 00:54:14,002
- Holmes is putting a
policeman in mechanic's clothes

1540
00:54:14,002 --> 00:54:16,516
with each gas station staying
open after ten at night.

1541
00:54:16,516 --> 00:54:17,349
- [Mike] Oh.

1542
00:54:17,349 --> 00:54:19,494
- [Dad] If the gang tries
another gas station heist,

1543
00:54:19,494 --> 00:54:21,567
They'll be all through.

1544
00:54:21,567 --> 00:54:22,980
- [Mike] Unless they hit liquor stores.

1545
00:54:22,980 --> 00:54:25,106
- Well, that's interesting
enough, for a starter.

1546
00:54:25,106 --> 00:54:27,236
- [Tom] Oh, by the way, have they built

1547
00:54:27,236 --> 00:54:29,403
any 7Eleven's around here?

1548
00:54:32,166 --> 00:54:33,098
- Bye.

1549
00:54:33,098 --> 00:54:34,411
- [Crow] What a neat kid.

1550
00:54:34,411 --> 00:54:36,994
Wish I had a daughter like her.

1551
00:54:39,731 --> 00:54:40,564
- [Tom] Come on girls,

1552
00:54:40,564 --> 00:54:42,905
let's spin donuts in the football field.

1553
00:54:42,905 --> 00:54:43,888
- [Crow] Ow, my pits hurt.

1554
00:54:43,888 --> 00:54:45,483
- I'm calling off the gas station jobs.

1555
00:54:45,483 --> 00:54:47,244
- Oh we can't call off Clanton's

1556
00:54:47,244 --> 00:54:48,077
- We already have.

1557
00:54:48,077 --> 00:54:48,910
- But why?

1558
00:54:48,910 --> 00:54:50,690
We coulda had more of a ball at Clantons,

1559
00:54:50,690 --> 00:54:52,499
than all the rest put together.

1560
00:54:52,499 --> 00:54:53,938
- Maybe I'd be a little
too much excitement,

1561
00:54:53,938 --> 00:54:55,571
even for us.

1562
00:54:55,571 --> 00:54:56,927
The cops have pulled sneak.

1563
00:54:56,927 --> 00:54:58,124
They'll be waiting for us

1564
00:54:58,124 --> 00:55:00,689
- So what, how can the cops
know we picked Clantons?

1565
00:55:00,689 --> 00:55:02,144
- I'll explain later.

1566
00:55:02,144 --> 00:55:03,843
The main thing is they know.

1567
00:55:03,843 --> 00:55:06,200
Puts an end to our gas
station jobs for a while.

1568
00:55:06,200 --> 00:55:08,143
We'll have to think up something else.

1569
00:55:08,143 --> 00:55:09,678
- [Crow] I hate robing retail.

1570
00:55:09,678 --> 00:55:11,324
- Think up something to get
back at Principle Bates.

1571
00:55:11,324 --> 00:55:12,910
- She have you on the carpet, again?

1572
00:55:12,910 --> 00:55:14,681
- Yeah, the old witch.

1573
00:55:14,681 --> 00:55:16,280
- What did you do this time?

1574
00:55:16,280 --> 00:55:17,798
- Practically nothing.

1575
00:55:17,798 --> 00:55:18,631
- [Mike] Killed a cop.

1576
00:55:18,631 --> 00:55:20,136
- Nothing for them to
get so hep up about it.

1577
00:55:20,136 --> 00:55:21,453
Just that math.

1578
00:55:21,453 --> 00:55:23,830
Teacher flunked me
again, so I told her off.

1579
00:55:23,830 --> 00:55:24,663
- [Mike] Wow.

1580
00:55:24,663 --> 00:55:25,927
- I'd like to get even with all of em.

1581
00:55:25,927 --> 00:55:27,990
- Maybe you'll get the chance sometime.

1582
00:55:27,990 --> 00:55:29,365
We'll have to go over to Shelia's tomorrow

1583
00:55:29,365 --> 00:55:31,261
and get rid a last weeks loot.

1584
00:55:31,261 --> 00:55:33,867
- [Crew] We hear and obey.

1585
00:55:33,867 --> 00:55:35,823
- How come we don't use
your car on our jobs?

1586
00:55:35,823 --> 00:55:37,262
How come your mother's all the time?

1587
00:55:37,262 --> 00:55:38,749
- My car would be spotted a mile a way.

1588
00:55:38,749 --> 00:55:39,832
Let's get in.

1589
00:55:42,129 --> 00:55:42,994
- [Crow] By the way, we're all

1590
00:55:42,994 --> 00:55:45,827
pregnant from the guy we attacked.

1591
00:55:46,975 --> 00:55:48,814
- [Tom] Ah, yes, the
scene that was condemned

1592
00:55:48,814 --> 00:55:51,639
by the Catholic League of America.

1593
00:55:51,639 --> 00:55:53,843
- [Crow] But I wanna rob Clanton's.

1594
00:55:53,843 --> 00:55:55,260
- [Mike] Shut up.

1595
00:55:56,973 --> 00:55:58,386
Hello?

1596
00:55:58,386 --> 00:55:59,512
- [Crow] They arrived in a u-boat.

1597
00:55:59,512 --> 00:56:01,735
They're all standing up in that car.

1598
00:56:01,735 --> 00:56:02,568
(laughs)

1599
00:56:02,568 --> 00:56:05,278
- [Tom] Oh, they have to
pick up Mamie Van Dorm.

1600
00:56:05,278 --> 00:56:06,753
- [Mike] That'd be hard.

1601
00:56:06,753 --> 00:56:11,072
- [Crow] Oh, phooey, it
says no solicitation.

1602
00:56:11,072 --> 00:56:12,905
Well, back in the car.

1603
00:56:13,921 --> 00:56:15,188
- [Tom] Okay, let's rob

1604
00:56:15,188 --> 00:56:18,083
the entire apartment building, instead.

1605
00:56:18,083 --> 00:56:22,801
- [Mike] Did anybody
remember the potato salad?

1606
00:56:22,801 --> 00:56:25,666
- [Crow] So, is this that Melrose Place

1607
00:56:25,666 --> 00:56:28,877
everyone's been talking about?

1608
00:56:28,877 --> 00:56:30,161
- [Tom] I know, they dress as men,

1609
00:56:30,161 --> 00:56:31,792
so they can live in
this apartment building.

1610
00:56:31,792 --> 00:56:33,245
- [Mike] (laughs) Nice.

1611
00:56:33,245 --> 00:56:34,685
(doorbell buzzes)

1612
00:56:34,685 --> 00:56:36,559
- [Crow] ♫ Walk like a man, talk like a ♫

1613
00:56:36,559 --> 00:56:37,392
Hello

1614
00:56:38,816 --> 00:56:40,219
- [Mike] Well, I don't know who you are,

1615
00:56:40,219 --> 00:56:43,136
or why you're here, but come on in.

1616
00:56:47,102 --> 00:56:49,157
[Tom] Sal Mineo entertains at home.

1617
00:56:49,157 --> 00:56:50,407
- You kids have been lucky.

1618
00:56:50,407 --> 00:56:53,171
- So what, maybe we're
just a little smart.

1619
00:56:53,171 --> 00:56:55,080
- Just be careful you don't get too smart

1620
00:56:55,080 --> 00:56:56,888
for your own good.

1621
00:56:56,888 --> 00:56:58,661
- [Mike] ♫ A double pleasure
is waiting for you ♫

1622
00:56:58,661 --> 00:56:59,994
- I'll get them.

1623
00:57:01,598 --> 00:57:04,560
- [Crow] Well, you look good.

1624
00:57:04,560 --> 00:57:06,812
- What have you got this time, more junk.

1625
00:57:06,812 --> 00:57:09,239
- We've never brought you junk, yet.

1626
00:57:09,239 --> 00:57:10,944
- Couldn't get back
half of what I paid you

1627
00:57:10,944 --> 00:57:12,298
for that last mess.

1628
00:57:12,298 --> 00:57:15,288
- Yeah, losing your money
get you a set up like this?

1629
00:57:15,288 --> 00:57:17,304
- [Mike] Isn't that Harpo on the right?

1630
00:57:17,304 --> 00:57:19,130
- [Tom] I think, uh-huh.

1631
00:57:19,130 --> 00:57:21,188
- Just as I thought, junk.

1632
00:57:21,188 --> 00:57:22,225
- Those are real diamonds.

1633
00:57:22,225 --> 00:57:23,146
I had them appraised.

1634
00:57:23,146 --> 00:57:24,104
- Appraised?

1635
00:57:24,104 --> 00:57:26,023
Well that was kind of foolish wasn't it?

1636
00:57:26,023 --> 00:57:27,649
You could be caught if they were traced.

1637
00:57:27,649 --> 00:57:29,083
- What do you take me for, a stoop?

1638
00:57:29,083 --> 00:57:29,918
- [Tom] No, a porch.

1639
00:57:29,918 --> 00:57:30,836
(laughs)

1640
00:57:30,836 --> 00:57:33,112
- He offered me $200
for the bracelet alone.

1641
00:57:33,112 --> 00:57:34,695
- $500 for the lot.

1642
00:57:35,611 --> 00:57:37,825
- I think we'll take
our business to Jimmy.

1643
00:57:37,825 --> 00:57:41,391
- [Crow] Ah, the free market at work.

1644
00:57:41,391 --> 00:57:44,033
- Come on gang, Shelia
doesn't wanna be bothered

1645
00:57:44,033 --> 00:57:45,134
with us anymore.

1646
00:57:45,134 --> 00:57:49,937
- [Tom] Let's take our
air fresheners elsewhere.

1647
00:57:49,937 --> 00:57:51,725
- You're a bunch of blackmailers.

1648
00:57:51,725 --> 00:57:53,418
- Oh, not that big, yet.

1649
00:57:53,418 --> 00:57:54,687
But give us a chance.

1650
00:57:54,687 --> 00:57:55,956
We're still growing.

1651
00:57:55,956 --> 00:57:57,921
- [Tom] We like milk, and it shows.

1652
00:57:57,921 --> 00:57:59,561
- Did you really see Jimmy this time?

1653
00:57:59,561 --> 00:58:01,004
- What difference does it make?

1654
00:58:01,004 --> 00:58:02,579
We got what we wanted.

1655
00:58:02,579 --> 00:58:04,462
I'm not interested in the money.

1656
00:58:04,462 --> 00:58:05,984
There's plenty of that at home.

1657
00:58:05,984 --> 00:58:07,894
It's the principle of the thing.

1658
00:58:07,894 --> 00:58:09,608
I don't want anyone getting the idea

1659
00:58:09,608 --> 00:58:11,901
they can put something over on us.

1660
00:58:11,901 --> 00:58:14,996
Shelia'd take our eye teeth
and then try to steal the rest.

1661
00:58:14,996 --> 00:58:17,259
It's the thrill that gets me.

1662
00:58:17,259 --> 00:58:19,245
The thrill of the chase made me.

1663
00:58:19,245 --> 00:58:20,078
- [Tom] Oh, and shopping.

1664
00:58:20,078 --> 00:58:21,328
I like to shop.

1665
00:58:23,161 --> 00:58:26,325
- [Crow] (gulping noises)
Oh, goodness, I like Squirt.

1666
00:58:26,325 --> 00:58:28,149
- [Mike] Ugh, here comes Uncle Mame.

1667
00:58:28,149 --> 00:58:32,419
- [Sheila] $750.00, you
think you're pretty smart.

1668
00:58:32,419 --> 00:58:33,457
Don't you?

1669
00:58:33,457 --> 00:58:34,992
- I don't think, I know.

1670
00:58:34,992 --> 00:58:36,600
That's why I'm the leader of this pack.

1671
00:58:36,600 --> 00:58:37,957
- You're a punk, or you wouldn't

1672
00:58:37,957 --> 00:58:40,812
be tied up in this small
time, penny ante business.

1673
00:58:40,812 --> 00:58:42,542
- You got better ideas?

1674
00:58:42,542 --> 00:58:43,375
- Maybe.

1675
00:58:44,581 --> 00:58:45,414
- Speak your piece.

1676
00:58:45,414 --> 00:58:46,640
- [Crow] Or forever hold you now.

1677
00:58:46,640 --> 00:58:49,495
- You can take the bottle
into the bedroom for a while.

1678
00:58:49,495 --> 00:58:51,836
I wanna talk to Paula, privately.

1679
00:58:51,836 --> 00:58:54,595
- [Tom] Uh, which one of us is Paula?

1680
00:58:54,595 --> 00:58:55,933
- And close the door.

1681
00:58:55,933 --> 00:58:57,613
- [Tom] Beat it Harpo, ah, ah.

1682
00:58:57,613 --> 00:58:58,945
- Start talking.

1683
00:58:58,945 --> 00:59:00,223
- [Tom] I just wanted to tell you,

1684
00:59:00,223 --> 00:59:02,640
how much I love your outfits.

1685
00:59:04,188 --> 00:59:06,713
- What do ya think about school?

1686
00:59:06,713 --> 00:59:08,840
- What are you driving at?

1687
00:59:08,840 --> 00:59:11,058
- I've got a connection
that doesn't like school.

1688
00:59:11,058 --> 00:59:11,891
- [Crow] Chester the third grader.

1689
00:59:11,891 --> 00:59:12,939
- Most kids don't.

1690
00:59:12,939 --> 00:59:14,697
- We're not talking kid talk.

1691
00:59:14,697 --> 00:59:18,149
It's worth a lot of money
to a certain organization

1692
00:59:18,149 --> 00:59:20,816
if certain damages are reported.

1693
00:59:21,826 --> 00:59:22,722
- I don't get it.

1694
00:59:22,722 --> 00:59:24,028
- You don't have to.

1695
00:59:24,028 --> 00:59:26,980
All you have to know is if you
can wreck a few school rooms,

1696
00:59:26,980 --> 00:59:29,908
you can make yourself, and
your pack, a load a dough.

1697
00:59:29,908 --> 00:59:30,796
- How much dough?

1698
00:59:30,796 --> 00:59:32,041
- More than you can use.

1699
00:59:32,041 --> 00:59:35,245
Besides, you've always said,
the money means nothing to you.

1700
00:59:35,245 --> 00:59:36,078
- [Tom] But it's about $10.00

1701
00:59:36,078 --> 00:59:38,075
- Why would anyone pay
to have schools wrecked?

1702
00:59:38,075 --> 00:59:40,233
- That's none of your business, or mine.

1703
00:59:40,233 --> 00:59:41,592
I have the connection.

1704
00:59:41,592 --> 00:59:43,803
And besides, there'll be
a great thrill behind this

1705
00:59:43,803 --> 00:59:46,711
move, and don't worry if a few flags

1706
00:59:46,711 --> 00:59:48,620
get destroyed in the process.

1707
00:59:48,620 --> 00:59:49,942
- [Tom] (gasps) Not Old Glory.

1708
00:59:49,942 --> 00:59:53,548
- Let's say it's part of a
well organized foreign plan.

1709
00:59:53,548 --> 00:59:55,119
- [Crow] Communism, whoa.

1710
00:59:55,119 --> 00:59:56,873
- You don't have to.

1711
00:59:56,873 --> 00:59:58,488
All you have to do is what you're told.

1712
00:59:58,488 --> 00:59:59,571
- [Crow] Now, go to your room.

1713
00:59:59,571 --> 01:00:00,404
- How soon do we move?

1714
01:00:00,404 --> 01:00:01,599
- Just as soon as you can.

1715
01:00:01,599 --> 01:00:02,978
But be careful you're not caught.

1716
01:00:02,978 --> 01:00:04,617
You're no good to me in the clink.

1717
01:00:04,617 --> 01:00:05,450
- [Tom] Clink.

1718
01:00:05,450 --> 01:00:06,745
- We won't get caught.

1719
01:00:06,745 --> 01:00:09,119
- I don't know how you can be so sure.

1720
01:00:09,119 --> 01:00:10,268
- [Crow] I wear Secret.

1721
01:00:10,268 --> 01:00:11,268
- It's easy.

1722
01:00:14,382 --> 01:00:18,982
- [Tom] This is one of the
rough spots in the dialectic.

1723
01:00:18,982 --> 01:00:20,252
- [Crow] Oh, Pete Hamill's place.

1724
01:00:20,252 --> 01:00:23,250
(upbeat jazz music)

1725
01:00:23,250 --> 01:00:25,759
- [Mike] They love this song, don't they.

1726
01:00:25,759 --> 01:00:27,318
- [Crow] Oh, look at those pajamas.

1727
01:00:27,318 --> 01:00:29,050
She may as well be nude.

1728
01:00:29,050 --> 01:00:30,411
- [Mike] Ah, the pajama pardigm.

1729
01:00:30,411 --> 01:00:32,824
- [Tom] I could use a hand here.

1730
01:00:32,824 --> 01:00:34,657
All right, who's next?

1731
01:00:35,890 --> 01:00:37,912
- [Mike] They'll strip in the
woods for a total stranger,

1732
01:00:37,912 --> 01:00:39,418
but they're modest in their own home.

1733
01:00:39,418 --> 01:00:41,369
(laughs)

1734
01:00:41,369 --> 01:00:42,729
- [Crow] Now there's a
girl who takes squishy-ness

1735
01:00:42,729 --> 01:00:45,862
and does something about it.

1736
01:00:45,862 --> 01:00:49,945
I think we're out of
s'mores, mmm, mmm, mmm, mmm.

1737
01:00:50,896 --> 01:00:52,915
- [Mike] Ooh, his pajamas
are a little formal.

1738
01:00:52,915 --> 01:00:54,902
- [Tom] Have you ever
thought of the advantages

1739
01:00:54,902 --> 01:00:58,445
of owning a truly fine
set of encyclopedias?

1740
01:00:58,445 --> 01:00:59,908
- [Crow] I wouldn't invite Ralph Bellamy

1741
01:00:59,908 --> 01:01:02,016
to my slumber party.

1742
01:01:02,016 --> 01:01:05,713
- [Tom] ♫ Thursday, Friday, happy days ♫

1743
01:01:05,713 --> 01:01:07,828
Your zipper seems to be stuck.

1744
01:01:07,828 --> 01:01:09,322
- You talk too much.

1745
01:01:09,322 --> 01:01:11,241
- Well all she did was
say you're the right guy,

1746
01:01:11,241 --> 01:01:13,000
and that I should meet you.

1747
01:01:13,000 --> 01:01:16,060
And as pretty as you are, I'm glad I came.

1748
01:01:16,060 --> 01:01:18,344
- I don't need her to
pick my friends for me.

1749
01:01:18,344 --> 01:01:19,177
- [Crow] Over my nose.

1750
01:01:19,177 --> 01:01:20,669
- I'm sure you don't.

1751
01:01:20,669 --> 01:01:22,040
- I'm pretty particular.

1752
01:01:22,040 --> 01:01:24,049
- Well I'm sure you are.

1753
01:01:24,049 --> 01:01:25,894
- I've played it alone for a long time.

1754
01:01:25,894 --> 01:01:26,727
- [Tom] Sure you have.

1755
01:01:26,727 --> 01:01:28,120
- That's how I plan to keep it.

1756
01:01:28,120 --> 01:01:30,721
- Maybe I should look for
my new company elsewhere.

1757
01:01:30,721 --> 01:01:33,200
- No, you're here now,
you might as well stay.

1758
01:01:33,200 --> 01:01:35,345
- Okay, since I'm now an invited guest,

1759
01:01:35,345 --> 01:01:38,238
you won't mind my giving
you a birthday gift.

1760
01:01:38,238 --> 01:01:40,699
- [Tom] Oh, I've already got one of these.

1761
01:01:40,699 --> 01:01:42,672
Do you have a receipt, so I can return it?

1762
01:01:42,672 --> 01:01:44,311
- [Crow] Oh heck, what's one more.

1763
01:01:44,311 --> 01:01:47,144
(loud jazz music)

1764
01:01:50,027 --> 01:01:54,431
- [Tom] ♫ Do anything but
lay off my blue suede shoes ♫

1765
01:01:54,431 --> 01:01:56,329
- [Crow] Pajamas, jazz and communism.

1766
01:01:56,329 --> 01:01:58,036
This is wild.

1767
01:01:58,036 --> 01:02:02,129
- [Tom] Oh, he's making
out with Erin Moran.

1768
01:02:02,129 --> 01:02:04,796
Someone's dancing to Otis.

1769
01:02:04,796 --> 01:02:06,615
- [Crow] Skin, I see skin.

1770
01:02:06,615 --> 01:02:07,681
- [Mike] Take it easy.

1771
01:02:07,681 --> 01:02:09,829
- [Crow] I don't, it's gone.

1772
01:02:09,829 --> 01:02:12,466
- [Tom] Masters and Johnson,
we've got some advice for you.

1773
01:02:12,466 --> 01:02:14,062
(loud knock)

1774
01:02:14,062 --> 01:02:17,500
- [Crow] Make-out police,
come out with your lips up.

1775
01:02:17,500 --> 01:02:18,333
- [Tom] Hi, you ready?

1776
01:02:18,333 --> 01:02:19,630
- [Manny] Yeah.

1777
01:02:19,630 --> 01:02:21,310
- I'd like to speak to Miss Parkins

1778
01:02:21,310 --> 01:02:22,374
- [Tom] Yeah.

1779
01:02:22,374 --> 01:02:23,207
- [Manny] Uh-huh.

1780
01:02:23,207 --> 01:02:24,040
- Yes.

1781
01:02:24,040 --> 01:02:25,022
- [Manny] What about?

1782
01:02:25,022 --> 01:02:26,035
- [Crow] Vinyl siding.

1783
01:02:26,035 --> 01:02:27,583
- I don't think that's any of your affair.

1784
01:02:27,583 --> 01:02:28,416
- Why you.

1785
01:02:28,416 --> 01:02:30,352
- It's all right, Manny.

1786
01:02:30,352 --> 01:02:31,877
He's from my dad's office.

1787
01:02:31,877 --> 01:02:34,963
- [Tom] Go ahead and punch him.

1788
01:02:34,963 --> 01:02:37,427
- Fond way to greet a
guest who bears gifts.

1789
01:02:37,427 --> 01:02:38,302
A mud butler.

1790
01:02:38,302 --> 01:02:39,135
- [Tom] What?

1791
01:02:39,135 --> 01:02:40,799
- Don't pay Manny a second thought.

1792
01:02:40,799 --> 01:02:42,907
He's seen too many gangster movies.

1793
01:02:42,907 --> 01:02:45,971
He's really very nice,
once you get to know him.

1794
01:02:45,971 --> 01:02:47,212
- That brings up the point.

1795
01:02:47,212 --> 01:02:48,754
Do many people stick around long enough

1796
01:02:48,754 --> 01:02:50,322
to get to know him that well?

1797
01:02:50,322 --> 01:02:51,219
- [Tom] Quit needling me.

1798
01:02:51,219 --> 01:02:52,353
- Can I come in?

1799
01:02:52,353 --> 01:02:54,230
- Sure if you want to.

1800
01:02:54,230 --> 01:02:55,591
- [Mike] Just let me
explain the mud butler joke.

1801
01:02:55,591 --> 01:02:58,093
See, a mud butler is a thing
that is placed in the ground,

1802
01:02:58,093 --> 01:03:00,394
bolted, and then you
scrape the mud off your...

1803
01:03:00,394 --> 01:03:01,651
You're not buying it.

1804
01:03:01,651 --> 01:03:03,725
- [Crow] Well, grab yourself a guy.

1805
01:03:03,725 --> 01:03:06,692
I'll see if I can find you a nightie.

1806
01:03:06,692 --> 01:03:09,995
- [Tom] I brought an extra lap.

1807
01:03:09,995 --> 01:03:11,906
- I see the party's formal dress.

1808
01:03:11,906 --> 01:03:13,606
- It's a pajama party.

1809
01:03:13,606 --> 01:03:14,723
- [Mike] I was joking again.

1810
01:03:14,723 --> 01:03:16,720
- Yeah, I can see that,
but mixed crowd, huh?

1811
01:03:16,720 --> 01:03:20,002
- Well ah, gang, I want you to meet

1812
01:03:20,002 --> 01:03:23,022
my father's star reporter, Barney Stetson.

1813
01:03:23,022 --> 01:03:23,950
- [Tom] Pajama party beat.

1814
01:03:23,950 --> 01:03:24,783
- Hi gang.

1815
01:03:26,277 --> 01:03:28,110
- [Tom] El seven, man.

1816
01:03:30,603 --> 01:03:32,515
- [Mike] What?

1817
01:03:32,515 --> 01:03:33,886
- Look, don't let me interrupt anything.

1818
01:03:33,886 --> 01:03:35,307
Birthdays are just like Christmas.

1819
01:03:35,307 --> 01:03:36,751
They only come once a year.

1820
01:03:36,751 --> 01:03:38,231
- [Crow] There is an
audible thud, every time

1821
01:03:38,231 --> 01:03:39,564
he tells a joke.

1822
01:03:43,512 --> 01:03:45,496
What am I alone in here?

1823
01:03:45,496 --> 01:03:46,741
Hello.

1824
01:03:46,741 --> 01:03:48,656
- It's kinda cold in
here, all of a sudden.

1825
01:03:48,656 --> 01:03:50,390
- [Crow] Again, a joke.
- Don't let it worry you.

1826
01:03:50,390 --> 01:03:54,223
- [Tom] I like to smoke,
do you like to smoke?

1827
01:03:55,994 --> 01:03:57,103
(gagging)

1828
01:03:57,103 --> 01:03:58,063
- That's a cute pair.

1829
01:03:58,063 --> 01:03:59,172
- [Crow] Thank you.

1830
01:03:59,172 --> 01:04:01,295
- They have their points.

1831
01:04:01,295 --> 01:04:02,813
- Oh, almost forgot the
reason for my visit.

1832
01:04:02,813 --> 01:04:04,652
Your father's going to
be very late tonight,

1833
01:04:04,652 --> 01:04:06,382
and asked me to drop this off for you.

1834
01:04:06,382 --> 01:04:07,215
- [Mike] Remote control?

1835
01:04:07,215 --> 01:04:09,207
- Poor Dad, he works so hard.

1836
01:04:09,207 --> 01:04:10,678
Thanks for bringing it.

1837
01:04:10,678 --> 01:04:11,996
- Aren't you gonna open it?

1838
01:04:11,996 --> 01:04:14,182
- Later, I know what it is.

1839
01:04:14,182 --> 01:04:17,333
Dad gets me the same
present every birthday.

1840
01:04:17,333 --> 01:04:18,246
- [Tom] A really little car.

1841
01:04:18,246 --> 01:04:19,876
- New watch.

1842
01:04:19,876 --> 01:04:21,320
Mom, come look.

1843
01:04:21,320 --> 01:04:22,704
- Oh, never mind, Paula.

1844
01:04:22,704 --> 01:04:25,749
I saw the new convertible when I came in.

1845
01:04:25,749 --> 01:04:26,940
- It never changes.

1846
01:04:26,940 --> 01:04:28,123
- [Crow] Like a mud butler?

1847
01:04:28,123 --> 01:04:29,792
- Mom and dad haven't been to
my birthday parties in years.

1848
01:04:29,792 --> 01:04:32,209
But always the same presents.

1849
01:04:33,151 --> 01:04:35,537
Mom sneaks out and takes
my old car in for a trade.

1850
01:04:35,537 --> 01:04:38,309
Then has a new one delivered secretly.

1851
01:04:38,309 --> 01:04:40,667
Takes a cab to her club.

1852
01:04:40,667 --> 01:04:43,520
Funny, I like her sedan better.

1853
01:04:43,520 --> 01:04:44,542
- [Mike] I have a really funny line here,

1854
01:04:44,542 --> 01:04:46,126
but I'm afraid to use it.

1855
01:04:46,126 --> 01:04:47,426
- Let's have some more music.

1856
01:04:47,426 --> 01:04:50,926
(upbeat electronic music)

1857
01:04:54,017 --> 01:04:55,265
- [Tom] Gotta love ya, Ada Annie.

1858
01:04:55,265 --> 01:04:57,783
- Ain't you see enough yet, sob sister?

1859
01:04:57,783 --> 01:05:00,306
Maybe you've seen enough
to know you ain't wanted.

1860
01:05:00,306 --> 01:05:02,242
- [Paula] Knock it off, Manny.

1861
01:05:02,242 --> 01:05:04,314
- I don't like this guy's looks.

1862
01:05:04,314 --> 01:05:05,220
- [Tom] Excuse me, I do.

1863
01:05:05,220 --> 01:05:07,463
- Why don't you change 'em, Manny.

1864
01:05:07,463 --> 01:05:10,013
- Maybe that's a good idea.

1865
01:05:10,013 --> 01:05:11,776
What about I should change
your looks, sob sister.

1866
01:05:11,776 --> 01:05:13,336
- [Tom] Mud butler.
- [Crow] Sob sister.

1867
01:05:13,336 --> 01:05:14,444
- [Tom] Mud butler.
- [Crow] Sob sister.

1868
01:05:14,444 --> 01:05:15,527
- [Mike] Ooh.

1869
01:05:16,504 --> 01:05:17,775
- How old are you, punk?

1870
01:05:17,775 --> 01:05:19,240
- Over 21.

1871
01:05:19,240 --> 01:05:20,640
- Well, that's old enough.

1872
01:05:20,640 --> 01:05:22,815
- [Tom] Wanna go out sometime?

1873
01:05:22,815 --> 01:05:24,005
(thud)

1874
01:05:24,005 --> 01:05:25,741
- [Tom] Thanks for stopping by, Ted.

1875
01:05:25,741 --> 01:05:27,408
- He asked for that.

1876
01:05:28,255 --> 01:05:30,557
- [Mike] I'm trying to think
of a quip or something here.

1877
01:05:30,557 --> 01:05:32,554
- You're in bad company, Paula.

1878
01:05:32,554 --> 01:05:35,194
But, I don't wanna sound like
I'm telling you your business.

1879
01:05:35,194 --> 01:05:36,526
- Then don't try.

1880
01:05:36,526 --> 01:05:38,270
- [Mike] Well, I'll see ya again next year

1881
01:05:38,270 --> 01:05:39,937
with your new watch.

1882
01:05:42,490 --> 01:05:45,906
- [Tom] How'd you guys
like that scene, hmm?

1883
01:05:45,906 --> 01:05:47,634
- [Mike] It's something
you'd wipe your boots on,

1884
01:05:47,634 --> 01:05:49,884
so it's an... oh forget it.

1885
01:05:52,820 --> 01:05:53,876
- [Crow] This is gonna
be the front page story

1886
01:05:53,876 --> 01:05:56,040
in the Glenview Shopper.

1887
01:05:56,040 --> 01:05:58,207
- [Tom] Change me, oh, ow.

1888
01:05:59,935 --> 01:06:04,102
- [Mike] We got trouble,
right here in River City.

1889
01:06:05,038 --> 01:06:06,421
- Why that dirty sob sister.

1890
01:06:06,421 --> 01:06:07,780
I'll take care of him.

1891
01:06:07,780 --> 01:06:10,593
- Haven't you had enough for one night?

1892
01:06:10,593 --> 01:06:12,117
- [Crow] This is one of the worst planned

1893
01:06:12,117 --> 01:06:14,063
pajama parties, ever.

1894
01:06:14,063 --> 01:06:16,123
- Good night, boys, the party's over.

1895
01:06:16,123 --> 01:06:19,012
- [Tom] Don't hate me Susan (cries softly)

1896
01:06:19,012 --> 01:06:21,385
- But the night's just beginning.

1897
01:06:21,385 --> 01:06:24,468
- [Crow] And that goes double for me.

1898
01:06:27,900 --> 01:06:31,567
Oh boy, I hope they
show another right turn.

1899
01:06:32,440 --> 01:06:36,314
(dramatic orchestra music)

1900
01:06:36,314 --> 01:06:38,981
Ha, they backed up the whole way

1901
01:06:40,095 --> 01:06:41,293
- [Tom] That's very good.

1902
01:06:41,293 --> 01:06:44,015
You scored a 94 on your driver's test.

1903
01:06:44,015 --> 01:06:48,182
- [Crow] Boomp, oh no, you hit
the crew and he's really mad.

1904
01:06:49,930 --> 01:06:52,348
- [Tom] Let us in, we
gotta make fruit fly media.

1905
01:06:52,348 --> 01:06:53,683
- This something I've been waiting for

1906
01:06:53,683 --> 01:06:55,319
for a long, long time.

1907
01:06:55,319 --> 01:06:57,227
- We'll get it done as
quickly as possible.

1908
01:06:57,227 --> 01:06:59,442
We'll get in, get the
job done, and get out.

1909
01:06:59,442 --> 01:07:03,609
- [Mike] Isn't it sort of
a general rule at a crime?

1910
01:07:06,983 --> 01:07:09,071
- [Crow] I hope they kill Mr. Kotter.

1911
01:07:09,071 --> 01:07:11,311
- I'm gonna smash
everything in this joint.

1912
01:07:11,311 --> 01:07:13,622
- Let's do it quick and get out of here.

1913
01:07:13,622 --> 01:07:18,011
- [Tom] They're going to bring
the 5th grade to its knees.

1914
01:07:18,011 --> 01:07:18,844
(crash)

1915
01:07:18,844 --> 01:07:20,209
- I hate you.

1916
01:07:20,209 --> 01:07:21,825
- [Tom] Stupid inanimate object.

1917
01:07:21,825 --> 01:07:24,276
- I wish this was your scrawny neck.

1918
01:07:24,276 --> 01:07:26,269
- [Mike] Good good,
you're letting it all out.

1919
01:07:26,269 --> 01:07:28,974
Now, what if both your parents were here?

1920
01:07:28,974 --> 01:07:32,097
- [Crew] Oh no, no, no not that, no.

1921
01:07:32,097 --> 01:07:34,393
- I'll hate you 'til the day I die.

1922
01:07:34,393 --> 01:07:37,678
- [Crow] And I'm not
gonna clap the erasers.

1923
01:07:37,678 --> 01:07:40,795
- [Tom] I'm making over $1.75 an hour.

1924
01:07:40,795 --> 01:07:41,628
(crash)

1925
01:07:41,628 --> 01:07:42,473
(shatter)

1926
01:07:42,473 --> 01:07:43,468
(bang)

1927
01:07:43,468 --> 01:07:46,902
- [Mike] This is actually
encouraged in open schools.

1928
01:07:46,902 --> 01:07:50,275
- [Crow] So, how is this
lucrative for them again?

1929
01:07:50,275 --> 01:07:52,525
- [Tom] I don't understand.

1930
01:07:54,360 --> 01:07:55,720
(clatter)

1931
01:07:55,720 --> 01:07:57,883
- [Crow] Girl gang goes on rampage.

1932
01:07:57,883 --> 01:08:00,892
President Eisenhower declares
a state of detention.

1933
01:08:00,892 --> 01:08:01,725
- [Tom] Down with the globe.

1934
01:08:01,725 --> 01:08:03,958
(shatters)

1935
01:08:03,958 --> 01:08:06,208
Mrs. Hathaway goes berserk.

1936
01:08:07,664 --> 01:08:09,400
This is for you, Betsy
Ross, you stupid, huh?

1937
01:08:09,400 --> 01:08:11,524
(siren)

1938
01:08:11,524 --> 01:08:13,607
Later, in completely unrelated footage.

1939
01:08:13,607 --> 01:08:15,361
(Mike laughs)

1940
01:08:15,361 --> 01:08:18,255
- [Mike] Oh, there,
different car, here we go.

1941
01:08:18,255 --> 01:08:19,901
- [Crow] Well, if it's
that denuding girl gang,

1942
01:08:19,901 --> 01:08:21,289
I'd better go in alone.

1943
01:08:21,289 --> 01:08:22,252
- We've all got guns.

1944
01:08:22,252 --> 01:08:23,189
Get 'em into action.

1945
01:08:23,189 --> 01:08:24,627
- [Mike] Just confess to
tipping the desks over.

1946
01:08:24,627 --> 01:08:25,597
Take your chances.

1947
01:08:25,597 --> 01:08:28,574
(gun fires)

1948
01:08:28,574 --> 01:08:30,217
(gun fires)

1949
01:08:30,217 --> 01:08:31,134
- They're shooting back.

1950
01:08:31,134 --> 01:08:31,967
- [Mike] What?

1951
01:08:31,967 --> 01:08:32,800
The bastards.

1952
01:08:32,800 --> 01:08:34,015
- What did you expect them
to do, throw powder puffs?

1953
01:08:34,015 --> 01:08:35,755
- Well, our ammunition can't last forever.

1954
01:08:35,755 --> 01:08:37,275
- How many have you all got?

1955
01:08:37,275 --> 01:08:38,466
- Whatever is in my gun.

1956
01:08:38,466 --> 01:08:40,123
- All I've got left is in my gun.

1957
01:08:40,123 --> 01:08:42,467
- I've got 10 shells in
my pocket, then poof.

1958
01:08:42,467 --> 01:08:43,659
We have to beat it to the car.

1959
01:08:43,659 --> 01:08:44,684
- [Crow] I've got gum.

1960
01:08:44,684 --> 01:08:46,614
- There's a dozen boxes
under the backseat.

1961
01:08:46,614 --> 01:08:47,800
Lay it into 'em fast,
and then we'll beat it.

1962
01:08:47,800 --> 01:08:49,010
- [Mike] They need to ask themselves

1963
01:08:49,010 --> 01:08:50,209
if this is really worth it.

1964
01:08:50,209 --> 01:08:51,254
- [Crow] Yeah.

1965
01:08:51,254 --> 01:08:54,286
(guns fire rapidly)

1966
01:08:54,286 --> 01:08:57,523
I'll distract them by
letting them assault me.

1967
01:08:57,523 --> 01:08:58,854
- Look at 'em jump.

1968
01:08:58,854 --> 01:09:00,354
Just like rabbits.

1969
01:09:01,334 --> 01:09:03,917
- [Crow] Rabbits with big guns.

1970
01:09:05,489 --> 01:09:06,572
And good aim.

1971
01:09:08,555 --> 01:09:09,611
- [Mike] Yes.

1972
01:09:09,611 --> 01:09:11,344
- It ain't
- [Mike] Isn't.

1973
01:09:11,344 --> 01:09:12,962
- supposed to be this way.

1974
01:09:12,962 --> 01:09:15,377
- [Crow] She died as she lived.

1975
01:09:15,377 --> 01:09:16,710
Failing algebra.

1976
01:09:18,263 --> 01:09:22,467
- [Tom] Just tell me where
the mascara is, aw jeez.

1977
01:09:22,467 --> 01:09:23,657
- Dirty.

1978
01:09:23,657 --> 01:09:25,484
- [Tom] Remember guys,
I once carved my name

1979
01:09:25,484 --> 01:09:27,375
in the bleachers and
there was a big shoot out.

1980
01:09:27,375 --> 01:09:28,208
- [Mike] Wow.

1981
01:09:28,208 --> 01:09:30,392
- [Tom] Whoopee, oh.

1982
01:09:30,392 --> 01:09:34,127
- [Crow] I think we're talking
about full expulsion, now.

1983
01:09:34,127 --> 01:09:35,995
- Save the rest of your ammo
and let's get out of here.

1984
01:09:35,995 --> 01:09:38,607
- [Tom] But we don't have hall passes.

1985
01:09:38,607 --> 01:09:40,016
(guns fire)

1986
01:09:40,016 --> 01:09:42,302
- [Mike] Grease, the
version they dared not make.

1987
01:09:42,302 --> 01:09:43,167
- Where do we go from here?

1988
01:09:43,167 --> 01:09:44,246
- [Crow] Softball practice.

1989
01:09:44,246 --> 01:09:46,306
- Shelia's, she got us into this mess.

1990
01:09:46,306 --> 01:09:48,243
And she can get us out.

1991
01:09:48,243 --> 01:09:49,715
- [Crow] Hey, they're cute.

1992
01:09:49,715 --> 01:09:52,054
- From now on, we gotta keep on the move.

