2241
01:18:31,693 --> 01:18:34,843
- Through this utter disregard for--

2242
01:18:34,843 --> 01:18:37,225
- [Tom] Stay awake, must stay wake.

2243
01:18:37,225 --> 01:18:41,392
- [Judge] Finds his eventual
end, a prisoner of the state.

2244
01:18:43,774 --> 01:18:45,435
- [Crow] You're mad, aren't you?

2245
01:18:45,435 --> 01:18:46,658
- [Judge] Standing in a court room,

2246
01:18:46,658 --> 01:18:50,094
convicted of murder, in the first degree.

2247
01:18:50,094 --> 01:18:51,529
- [Crow] Look, I gotta get
going on my life sentence

2248
01:18:51,529 --> 01:18:52,769
Speed it up here.

2249
01:18:52,769 --> 01:18:55,304
- Because of your youth, Paula Parkins,

2250
01:18:55,304 --> 01:18:57,519
it's impossible for me to give you

2251
01:18:57,519 --> 01:19:00,950
the sentence you so rightfully deserve.

2252
01:19:00,950 --> 01:19:02,919
- [Crow] So you get the spanking machine.

2253
01:19:02,919 --> 01:19:06,907
- So you are hereby manded
to the proper institution.

2254
01:19:06,907 --> 01:19:08,257
- [Tom] Me.

2255
01:19:08,257 --> 01:19:10,431
- Until you become 21 years of age.

2256
01:19:10,431 --> 01:19:12,048
- [Tom] Then we party, whoo.

2257
01:19:12,048 --> 01:19:14,902
- At which time you will be transferred

2258
01:19:14,902 --> 01:19:17,051
to the state penitentiary for women.

2259
01:19:17,051 --> 01:19:19,354
- [Crow] And get a shave
while you're at it.

2260
01:19:19,354 --> 01:19:23,053
- Where you will spend the
rest of your natural life.

2261
01:19:23,053 --> 01:19:24,452
- [Tom] How's that sound?

2262
01:19:24,452 --> 01:19:27,891
(dramatic orchestra music)

2263
01:19:27,891 --> 01:19:30,089
Oh, sure screwed up on that daughter, huh.

2264
01:19:30,089 --> 01:19:33,738
- [Crow] Wow, I feel kinda
bad, I have to admit.

2265
01:19:33,738 --> 01:19:38,244
You know it's actually
a very nice, sunny cell.

2266
01:19:38,244 --> 01:19:41,244
- [Mike] From one prison to another.

2267
01:19:43,589 --> 01:19:47,939
- [Crow] It's too late to
spend time with her, isn't it?

2268
01:19:47,939 --> 01:19:50,277
- I don't think I could drink another cup.

2269
01:19:50,277 --> 01:19:51,748
- It passes the time.

2270
01:19:51,748 --> 01:19:53,150
- [Tom] Do you think I
look like Lincoln, honey.

2271
01:19:53,150 --> 01:19:55,163
- It's all my fault.

2272
01:19:55,163 --> 01:19:58,237
If only I hadn't thought
more of my outside interests

2273
01:19:58,237 --> 01:19:59,799
than I did of Paula.

2274
01:19:59,799 --> 01:20:01,798
- [Tom] It's okay if I think
it's your fault, too, isn't it?

2275
01:20:01,798 --> 01:20:03,780
- What a strong, hard
lesson we've been taught.

2276
01:20:03,780 --> 01:20:04,832
- [Crow] Live and learn.

2277
01:20:04,832 --> 01:20:07,534
- It's no more your fault than it is mine.

2278
01:20:07,534 --> 01:20:10,140
We're certainly a fine pair of parents.

2279
01:20:10,140 --> 01:20:11,468
- We still have each other.

2280
01:20:11,468 --> 01:20:12,729
That's some comfort.

2281
01:20:12,729 --> 01:20:14,312
- [Mike] That supposed to be funny?

2282
01:20:14,312 --> 01:20:16,676
- It all seems like such a bad dream.

2283
01:20:16,676 --> 01:20:18,684
- [Mike] Oh, like when you're
naked at a hockey game.

2284
01:20:18,684 --> 01:20:22,255
- Ever since I can remember,
we've given Paula everything.

2285
01:20:22,255 --> 01:20:23,947
Everything but real love.

2286
01:20:23,947 --> 01:20:25,653
- [Crow] And peanut butter.

2287
01:20:25,653 --> 01:20:29,225
- A new dress, instead of a caress.

2288
01:20:29,225 --> 01:20:30,461
A new car--

2289
01:20:30,461 --> 01:20:32,097
- [Tom] Instead of a cigar.

2290
01:20:32,097 --> 01:20:33,983
- Heart talk.

2291
01:20:33,983 --> 01:20:35,111
A new watch.

2292
01:20:35,111 --> 01:20:36,907
- [Tom] Instead of a squash.

2293
01:20:36,907 --> 01:20:38,876
- It does no good to look back.

2294
01:20:38,876 --> 01:20:40,695
It can only be more of a hurt.

2295
01:20:40,695 --> 01:20:42,528
We must now look forward.

2296
01:20:42,528 --> 01:20:43,970
- [Crow] Let's get a goldfish.

2297
01:20:43,970 --> 01:20:45,574
- Reliving the past is only a pattern

2298
01:20:45,574 --> 01:20:48,328
of judgement for the future.

2299
01:20:48,328 --> 01:20:50,930
Paula's lesson to us was strong.

2300
01:20:50,930 --> 01:20:51,875
- [Tom] Instead of a thong.

2301
01:20:51,875 --> 01:20:55,625
- But, her child will
profit by our mistakes.

2302
01:20:56,713 --> 01:20:58,564
- [Tom] By the way, you seen her lately?

2303
01:20:58,564 --> 01:20:59,617
I haven't had a chance to get up there,

2304
01:20:59,617 --> 01:21:02,454
with all the work I got and everything.

2305
01:21:02,454 --> 01:21:05,621
(sad orchestra music)

2306
01:21:07,662 --> 01:21:09,252
- [Mike] We can only
stay for a minute, right?

2307
01:21:09,252 --> 01:21:11,085
And what's our excuse?

2308
01:21:12,616 --> 01:21:16,116
(upbeat electronic music)

2309
01:21:20,041 --> 01:21:22,421
- [Crow] Oh hi, Peggy, P-P-P_Paula.

2310
01:21:22,421 --> 01:21:24,478
That's it, Paula.

2311
01:21:24,478 --> 01:21:25,584
- How do you feel, darling.

2312
01:21:25,584 --> 01:21:27,792
- [Tom] Good, real good.

2313
01:21:27,792 --> 01:21:31,833
- I don't want my baby
in a place like this.

2314
01:21:31,833 --> 01:21:32,666
I don't.

2315
01:21:36,612 --> 01:21:38,885
I don't feel so well.

2316
01:21:38,885 --> 01:21:41,964
Everything keeps going around, and--

2317
01:21:41,964 --> 01:21:44,223
- We'll see Judge Clara.

2318
01:21:44,223 --> 01:21:46,020
We'll take care of everything.

2319
01:21:46,020 --> 01:21:47,944
- [Crow] C'mon let's go,
let's just go, c'mon.

2320
01:21:47,944 --> 01:21:49,716
- If only we knew who--

2321
01:21:49,716 --> 01:21:52,212
- Everything will be all right.

2322
01:21:52,212 --> 01:21:56,379
- [Tom] My rape victim refuses
to come to Lamaze classes.

2323
01:21:59,683 --> 01:22:02,034
Well, how's our little gun toting trollop?

2324
01:22:02,034 --> 01:22:03,117
- [Mike] Shh.

2325
01:22:05,664 --> 01:22:07,694
- That's all now, it's
time you were leaving.

2326
01:22:07,694 --> 01:22:09,685
- [Crow] I'm afraid she's passed away.

2327
01:22:09,685 --> 01:22:11,232
- Rest easy now.

2328
01:22:11,232 --> 01:22:13,442
It'll be all right.

2329
01:22:13,442 --> 01:22:15,605
- We'll see you when it's all over.

2330
01:22:15,605 --> 01:22:18,063
- [Tom] Sometime after
Memorial Day, maybe.

2331
01:22:18,063 --> 01:22:22,230
- [Crow] Look, I'll, uh, send
my secretary to see the baby.

2332
01:22:24,945 --> 01:22:27,945
She's got a room on the 400th floor.

2333
01:22:31,275 --> 01:22:33,942
- [Tom] Regrets, I've had a few.

2334
01:22:39,519 --> 01:22:40,352
- So what.

2335
01:22:42,060 --> 01:22:43,716
- [Mike] Hey, what's her deal?

2336
01:22:43,716 --> 01:22:45,549
- [Crow] Leave us out.

2337
01:22:47,881 --> 01:22:50,048
Here on surgery boulevard.

2338
01:22:51,814 --> 01:22:56,600
- [Tom] I'm teeing off at
three, that's all I'm saying.

2339
01:22:56,600 --> 01:23:00,183
Maybe I could fund this
as a charity event.

2340
01:23:03,341 --> 01:23:04,674
It's your fault.

2341
01:23:08,028 --> 01:23:09,876
- [Crow] Now, I'm going to be a bit ugly,

2342
01:23:09,876 --> 01:23:11,381
so you'd better sit down.

2343
01:23:11,381 --> 01:23:12,348
- [Mike] Wow.

2344
01:23:12,348 --> 01:23:17,053
- [Tom] How many arms did she
have when she came in here?

2345
01:23:17,053 --> 01:23:20,386
- The baby, a girl, is well and healthy.

2346
01:23:21,619 --> 01:23:24,450
- My daughter, how is she?

2347
01:23:24,450 --> 01:23:26,700
- [Tom] I broke her, sorry.

2348
01:23:28,316 --> 01:23:29,800
- She, she died.

2349
01:23:29,800 --> 01:23:32,353
(mom gasps)

2350
01:23:32,353 --> 01:23:34,211
- [Crow] Well, so the life
sentence really wasn't that long.

2351
01:23:34,211 --> 01:23:35,937
- [Tom] Not too bad, oh easy to do.

2352
01:23:35,937 --> 01:23:37,687
- [Crow] Pretty good.

2353
01:23:39,958 --> 01:23:41,938
- [Judge] Some people
think newspapers exaggerate

2354
01:23:41,938 --> 01:23:43,149
juvenile crime.

2355
01:23:43,149 --> 01:23:44,384
- [Tom] We don't can we go?

2356
01:23:44,384 --> 01:23:46,748
- Or that it's confined
mostly to large cities.

2357
01:23:46,748 --> 01:23:48,027
- [Mike] Oh, dear god.

2358
01:23:48,027 --> 01:23:50,659
- This is far from being the case.

2359
01:23:50,659 --> 01:23:53,583
From coast to coast, in small communities,

2360
01:23:53,583 --> 01:23:55,420
as well as the larger cities--

2361
01:23:55,420 --> 01:23:56,491
- [Crow] Even Circle Pines.

2362
01:23:56,491 --> 01:23:58,765
- Juvenile delinquency is on the rise.

2363
01:23:58,765 --> 01:24:01,319
- [Tom] I'm playing fast and
loose with my numbers, here.

2364
01:24:01,319 --> 01:24:03,423
- No child is inherently bad.

2365
01:24:03,423 --> 01:24:05,462
- [Tom] Except Pauly Shore.

2366
01:24:05,462 --> 01:24:07,688
- He's made what he is by his upbringing,

2367
01:24:07,688 --> 01:24:08,798
and his surroundings.

2368
01:24:08,798 --> 01:24:11,540
- [Crow] And his inherently bad parents.

2369
01:24:11,540 --> 01:24:13,709
- As a parent, that
something has gone wrong

2370
01:24:13,709 --> 01:24:17,027
with the environment of a
great many of these children.

2371
01:24:17,027 --> 01:24:18,362
- [Mike] Just leave.

2372
01:24:18,362 --> 01:24:21,444
- Or we wouldn't have this
present delinquency problem.

2373
01:24:21,444 --> 01:24:24,012
Adults create the world children live in.

2374
01:24:24,012 --> 01:24:25,207
- [Mike] End.

2375
01:24:25,207 --> 01:24:29,578
- In this process,
parents pay the key role.

2376
01:24:29,578 --> 01:24:31,726
When children grow up among adults.

2377
01:24:31,726 --> 01:24:32,559
(crew sighs)

2378
01:24:32,559 --> 01:24:35,206
Who refuse to recognise anything that is

2379
01:24:35,206 --> 01:24:38,974
fine and good, or worthy of respect.

2380
01:24:38,974 --> 01:24:40,601
- [Tom] There's a little
joke in Leviticus,

2381
01:24:40,601 --> 01:24:41,853
that says--

2382
01:24:41,853 --> 01:24:44,566
- Many of these children, fail to develop

2383
01:24:44,566 --> 01:24:48,480
high moral standards, or to
distinguish right from wrong.

2384
01:24:48,480 --> 01:24:50,965
- [Mike] Why don't you wrap it
up, judge pedantic, come on.

2385
01:24:50,965 --> 01:24:53,142
- I can sum it up in two callous words.

2386
01:24:53,142 --> 01:24:54,558
- [Crow] Bore-ing.

2387
01:24:54,558 --> 01:24:55,834
- So what.

2388
01:24:55,834 --> 01:25:00,001
Juvenile delinquency is always
rooted in adult delinquency.

2389
01:25:01,464 --> 01:25:02,734
- [Tom] Why don't you shut up?

2390
01:25:02,734 --> 01:25:04,067
Shut up, shut up, shut up,
shut up, shut up, shut up.

2391
01:25:04,067 --> 01:25:05,004
(Tom breaks down wailing)

2392
01:25:05,004 --> 01:25:07,451
- Through higher moral values

2393
01:25:07,451 --> 01:25:09,202
can we hope to solve the problem.

2394
01:25:09,202 --> 01:25:10,469
- [Mike] Ah, look, we're double parked.

2395
01:25:10,469 --> 01:25:12,529
- The easiest way to bring this about

2396
01:25:12,529 --> 01:25:14,055
is through a return to religion.

2397
01:25:14,055 --> 01:25:16,463
- [Tom] Oh, excuse me,
separation of church and state?

2398
01:25:16,463 --> 01:25:17,878
- If all people would join this

2399
01:25:17,878 --> 01:25:20,985
back to god movement,
and train their children

2400
01:25:20,985 --> 01:25:23,310
to respect the 10 commandments--

2401
01:25:23,310 --> 01:25:26,059
- [Tom] Beat the love of Jesus into them.

2402
01:25:26,059 --> 01:25:29,162
- Handed down by all the great religions.

2403
01:25:29,162 --> 01:25:31,761
- [Crow] Your Catholicism,
your Poligma of Thorton Eight.

2404
01:25:31,761 --> 01:25:35,928
- The time has come that, we
must impose sterner penalties.

2405
01:25:36,991 --> 01:25:40,985
- [Tom] ♫ Get a picture of
the smiles we left behind ♫

2406
01:25:40,985 --> 01:25:42,725
- [Judge] Against the young law breakers.

2407
01:25:42,725 --> 01:25:45,020
- [Crow] Hi, I was, again?

2408
01:25:45,020 --> 01:25:48,103
- [Judge] His mere youth is no excuse

2409
01:25:48,958 --> 01:25:51,808
for letting him remain at large.

2410
01:25:51,808 --> 01:25:55,975
(Crew loudly hums "Battle
Hymn of the Republic")

2411
01:25:57,426 --> 01:25:58,711
- [Judge] Dangerous law breakers.

2412
01:25:58,711 --> 01:25:59,544
- [Crow] But, some crimes are good.

2413
01:25:59,544 --> 01:26:01,012
- [Mike] Oh yeah.

2414
01:26:01,012 --> 01:26:02,467
- [Judge] A new young offender should be

2415
01:26:02,467 --> 01:26:04,611
released in the custody of his parents,

2416
01:26:04,611 --> 01:26:06,667
unless an investigation
shows the parents are--

2417
01:26:06,667 --> 01:26:09,167
- [Mike] I can't see this
scene too many times.

2418
01:26:09,167 --> 01:26:13,669
- [Judge] If they are not,
then supervision should be

2419
01:26:13,669 --> 01:26:16,392
turned over to a more responsible agency.

2420
01:26:16,392 --> 01:26:19,521
- [Tom] Booze and jammies don't mix.

2421
01:26:19,521 --> 01:26:22,100
- [Judge] And in making
parents financially liable

2422
01:26:22,100 --> 01:26:25,051
for property damaged
caused by their children,

2423
01:26:25,051 --> 01:26:27,714
could be especially
helpful against the wave

2424
01:26:27,714 --> 01:26:32,134
of vandalism which has been
sweeping across our country.

2425
01:26:32,134 --> 01:26:34,689
- [Crow] Even this adorable vandalism.

2426
01:26:34,689 --> 01:26:35,890
- [Judge] For this responsibility,

2427
01:26:35,890 --> 01:26:38,890
we're sure that parents
would keep a closer eye

2428
01:26:38,890 --> 01:26:41,110
on the kind of fun their
children are having.

2429
01:26:41,110 --> 01:26:42,458
- [Tom] Think fast, window.

2430
01:26:42,458 --> 01:26:44,684
- [Judge] And if the
old fashioned woodshed

2431
01:26:44,684 --> 01:26:48,037
would receive more use,
vandalism would decline.

2432
01:26:48,037 --> 01:26:49,506
- [Mike] I have no real proof,

2433
01:26:49,506 --> 01:26:51,324
but it's worth a try, what the hell.

2434
01:26:51,324 --> 01:26:53,261
- [Judge] Some parents refuse to recognize

2435
01:26:53,261 --> 01:26:55,009
the moral obligation--

2436
01:26:55,009 --> 01:26:56,354
- [Crow] Count juggula.

2437
01:26:56,354 --> 01:26:58,316
- [Mike] (laughs) Wow.

2438
01:26:58,316 --> 01:27:00,145
- [Judge] When that is the case,

2439
01:27:00,145 --> 01:27:01,903
we must take stronger measures to

2440
01:27:01,903 --> 01:27:04,444
make them realize their responsibilities.

2441
01:27:04,444 --> 01:27:06,350
- [Crow] Oh, my body.

2442
01:27:06,350 --> 01:27:09,999
- So you see, I cannot possibly grant your

2443
01:27:09,999 --> 01:27:12,300
request for the adoption of
the child of your daughter.

2444
01:27:12,300 --> 01:27:14,431
The child must remain a ward of the state.

2445
01:27:14,431 --> 01:27:16,445
- [Tom] A Burt Ward.

2446
01:27:16,445 --> 01:27:19,236
- Until such time as a
suitable family can be found.

2447
01:27:19,236 --> 01:27:22,578
At that time, the child will be granted

2448
01:27:22,578 --> 01:27:25,871
to this worthy family to
be brought up in the world

2449
01:27:25,871 --> 01:27:28,963
as it should know the world.

2450
01:27:28,963 --> 01:27:30,748
- [Crow] The warm glow of a state home.

2451
01:27:30,748 --> 01:27:32,235
- For you Jane Parkins

2452
01:27:32,235 --> 01:27:33,157
- [Tom] Take this man to be your--

2453
01:27:33,157 --> 01:27:35,244
- And for you, Carl Parkins,

2454
01:27:35,244 --> 01:27:36,606
- [Tom] To love, cherish, and obey--

2455
01:27:36,606 --> 01:27:39,984
- I can only feel the deepest of sorrow.

2456
01:27:39,984 --> 01:27:43,024
- [Crow] I sentence you to
my Toastmasters meeting.

2457
01:27:43,024 --> 01:27:44,156
(dramatic sad orchestra music)

2458
01:27:44,156 --> 01:27:46,656
- [Tom] Hey, hey, hey,
where are you two going?

2459
01:27:46,656 --> 01:27:50,114
Will all the jerkwads please
report to the bathroom.

2460
01:27:50,114 --> 01:27:52,780
Thank you, tonight on
the Hollywood Squares.

2461
01:27:52,780 --> 01:27:55,022
(baby cries)

2462
01:27:55,022 --> 01:27:56,619
Wah, wha, wha, I'm an infant.

2463
01:27:56,619 --> 01:27:57,615
I'm an infant.

2464
01:27:57,615 --> 01:27:58,927
Give me everything.

2465
01:27:58,927 --> 01:28:01,569
(baby cries)

2466
01:28:01,569 --> 01:28:02,950
- [Mike] Boy fun, huh?

2467
01:28:02,950 --> 01:28:06,657
- [Crow] This was the
feel good move of 1956.

2468
01:28:06,657 --> 01:28:08,388
- [Tom] Audiences left with
a song in their hearts,

2469
01:28:08,388 --> 01:28:11,449
and a smile on their faces.

2470
01:28:11,449 --> 01:28:14,699
(loud orchestra music)

2471
01:28:16,782 --> 01:28:19,199
(door slams)

2472
01:28:28,819 --> 01:28:30,635
(whirring noise)

2473
01:28:30,635 --> 01:28:32,337
- Dum-dum-da-dum-do, whoa.

2474
01:28:32,337 --> 01:28:34,003
- Oh, what are they doin'?

2475
01:28:34,003 --> 01:28:35,247
- I think they're recreating

2476
01:28:35,247 --> 01:28:37,423
the gas station hold up from the movie.

2477
01:28:37,423 --> 01:28:38,256
- Oh.

2478
01:28:38,256 --> 01:28:39,266
- Let's watch.

2479
01:28:39,266 --> 01:28:42,016
(whirring noise)

2480
01:28:44,698 --> 01:28:45,531
- Wow.

2481
01:28:46,592 --> 01:28:47,425
- Huh.

2482
01:28:51,126 --> 01:28:54,728
(soft dinging sound)

2483
01:28:54,728 --> 01:28:55,561
- Huh.

2484
01:28:58,150 --> 01:29:01,233
